1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,560

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,080
Now let's get started.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:10,560
And today we will be finishing
up model-based reinforcement

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,280
learning, specifically a
way to use a learned model

5
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:16,239
with synthetic data
generation and when

6
00:00:16,239 --> 00:00:18,480
to use model-based
reinforcement learning.

7
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:20,640
And then we're going to
be talking about multitask

8
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:22,360
imitation learning
and reinforcement

9
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:27,760
learning, including ways
to conditions on tasks,

10
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:30,320
different tasks as
goal-reaching tasks,

11
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:33,100
and an approach called
hindsight relabeling,

12
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:35,600
which you'll be implementing.

13
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,560
Cool.

14
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:40,020
So let's get back to model-based
reinforcement learning.

15
00:00:40,020 --> 00:00:42,960
So last time, we
talked about a scenario

16
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:46,160
where we learned some model.

17
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:48,300
That model could be a
deterministic model.

18
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:55,000
It could also be modeling a
distribution over next states.

19
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,040
And in particular, you
could think about it

20
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:02,950
as trying to approximate
the distribution

21
00:01:02,950 --> 00:01:07,590
over the next state, given
the current state and action.

22
00:01:07,590 --> 00:01:12,230
And you can use this
model to actually predict

23
00:01:12,230 --> 00:01:13,730
what will happen in the future.

24
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:17,510
And for example, if you have
an action that you think

25
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:19,150
you might take or
multiple actions

26
00:01:19,150 --> 00:01:20,630
you think you
might take, you can

27
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:26,910
use this estimated distribution
to generate or imagine

28
00:01:26,910 --> 00:01:28,790
what might happen
in the future if you

29
00:01:28,790 --> 00:01:32,510
were to take those actions.

30
00:01:32,510 --> 00:01:36,110
We talked about an
approach to behavior

31
00:01:36,110 --> 00:01:38,390
that's quite different
from learning a policy,

32
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:40,990
where we're not actually
explicitly learning

33
00:01:40,990 --> 00:01:44,950
a mapping from states to
actions and instead, on the fly,

34
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:47,950
we are thinking through,
for a given state,

35
00:01:47,950 --> 00:01:49,470
what actions we should take.

36
00:01:49,470 --> 00:01:51,150
And we called this planning.

37
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:53,470
And we talked about how
this sort of planning

38
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:57,630
can be done with gradient-based
or sampling-based optimization

39
00:01:57,630 --> 00:02:00,813
over the next future horizon.

40
00:02:00,813 --> 00:02:02,230
Then we also talked
about how it's

41
00:02:02,230 --> 00:02:05,670
important to update the model
using the data collected

42
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:08,030
because then you'll be
able to actually learn

43
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:12,928
a better model in the realms
that you want to use that model.

44
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:14,470
And then periodically,
we also talked

45
00:02:14,470 --> 00:02:17,850
about how you can replan to
help account for mistakes,

46
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,270
account for errors in
the model, and also react

47
00:02:22,270 --> 00:02:25,790
to changes in the environment.

48
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:26,360
Cool.

49
00:02:26,360 --> 00:02:28,110
The pros of this sort
of planning approach

50
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:29,130
is that it's simple.

51
00:02:29,130 --> 00:02:31,910
It's easy to plug in
different goals or rewards

52
00:02:31,910 --> 00:02:34,550
if you have a general
enough model, possibly

53
00:02:34,550 --> 00:02:36,030
even at test time.

54
00:02:36,030 --> 00:02:39,130
And the downside is that it's
compute intensive at test time.

55
00:02:39,130 --> 00:02:42,470
It requires a lot of test time
compute at inference time.

56
00:02:42,470 --> 00:02:45,110
And this particular
instantiation

57
00:02:45,110 --> 00:02:49,190
was only practical for
short-horizon problems

58
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:52,470
or for very shaped rewards,
where you can plan over

59
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:55,870
some shorter horizon
rather than considering

60
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:57,570
an extremely long horizon.

61
00:02:57,570 --> 00:03:01,500

62
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,940
And there's two reasons
why this is only

63
00:03:04,940 --> 00:03:06,200
applicable to short horizons.

64
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,580
One is that as you
increase the horizon,

65
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:11,040
it becomes even more
compute intensive.

66
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:13,860
And the second reason is that
the model is not very accurate

67
00:03:13,860 --> 00:03:17,860
when you try to apply it
multiple times to generate

68
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:21,020
a long trajectory.

69
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:21,910
Cool.

70
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:23,660
Now, there's a second
way that you can use

71
00:03:23,660 --> 00:03:25,340
these kinds of learned models.

72
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:28,420
And that is to learn a policy.

73
00:03:28,420 --> 00:03:31,940
And so that's what we'll
talk about right now.

74
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:33,820
And the key idea is
that we can actually

75
00:03:33,820 --> 00:03:38,100
use this model of the future,
this kind of learned simulator,

76
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:42,620
to augment any sort of
data that we've collected.

77
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:45,840
So say that in the real world
or the real environment,

78
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,460
we've collected some data.

79
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,400
So we have some trajectories.

80
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,780
Some of this data could also
have been provided to us

81
00:03:52,780 --> 00:03:55,340
in an offline data set.

82
00:03:55,340 --> 00:04:01,130
And so maybe the data that we
have in the real environment

83
00:04:01,130 --> 00:04:04,610
looks something like this.

84
00:04:04,610 --> 00:04:11,130
Does anyone have
thoughts on ways

85
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:15,970
in which we might want to use
our learned model to augment

86
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:19,490
this data set right here?

87
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:22,050
Like, procedurally,
what would we

88
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:25,150
do to try to generate
synthetic data?

89
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:25,650
Yeah.

90
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:28,102
So the thought there was
that you could take--

91
00:04:28,102 --> 00:04:30,053
you said you could
take the last state

92
00:04:30,053 --> 00:04:31,470
and then generate
more from there.

93
00:04:31,470 --> 00:04:37,410
So you could add on to
the last state here.

94
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:39,007
So that's one
thing you could do.

95
00:04:39,007 --> 00:04:40,590
Other thoughts on
things you could do?

96
00:04:40,590 --> 00:04:41,850
Yeah?

97
00:04:41,850 --> 00:04:44,888
So something like dagger, where
you take the first part that's

98
00:04:44,888 --> 00:04:46,930
run by the policy and then
the second part that's

99
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:47,905
from the model.

100
00:04:47,905 --> 00:04:49,530
So it's kind of
similar to this, maybe.

101
00:04:49,530 --> 00:04:50,910
But maybe you do it earlier.

102
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:52,470
Is that what you're saying?

103
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:52,970
Yeah.

104
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:56,620
So you don't just have
to start at the end.

105
00:04:56,620 --> 00:04:59,580
You can also start anywhere
in the middle as well.

106
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,280
So you could, for example,
generate trajectories

107
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:07,600
from, really, any sort
of state in your buffer,

108
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:09,040
in your data set.

109
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,720
And then if you were to
do something like that,

110
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:17,360
then you would be
able to generate

111
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:20,640
data that looks like this,
where everything that is in red

112
00:05:20,640 --> 00:05:27,080
is synthetic data or data that
was imagined using your model.

113
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:31,280
And then everything in
black is the real data

114
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,120
from the environment.

115
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,480
Now, one thing that
we talked about before

116
00:05:35,480 --> 00:05:40,808
is that our model is
not very good to be--

117
00:05:40,808 --> 00:05:42,600
like, you can't really
trust it if you were

118
00:05:42,600 --> 00:05:44,480
to make a really long rollout.

119
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:47,600
And so one thing that
you can do is keep

120
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:49,920
these trajectories very short.

121
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:53,960
And so instead of having
these be almost as

122
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:55,820
long as your original
trajectories,

123
00:05:55,820 --> 00:06:02,640
you could actually change them
to be just little short rollouts

124
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:06,120
that look something like
this and that basically

125
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:10,320
add a little bit more
coverage around the trajectory

126
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:11,640
in your data.

127
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:16,840
And so that would look
something like this.

128
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:20,680
And something like this
picture is often more

129
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,280
like what people
find works really

130
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:31,480
well in practice because of
inaccuracies in your model.

131
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:35,140
And so specifically--
say we have some example,

132
00:06:35,140 --> 00:06:36,880
real trajectory.

133
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,840
Then maybe the first
way that you could

134
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,958
think about creating data--

135
00:06:41,958 --> 00:06:44,500
maybe it wasn't the first way
that you guys thought about it.

136
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:45,440
But one simple
thing you could do

137
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:47,080
is just start from
initial states

138
00:06:47,080 --> 00:06:49,160
and generate full
trajectories as if it

139
00:06:49,160 --> 00:06:51,120
were the real environment.

140
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:53,230
But the downside there
is that the model may not

141
00:06:53,230 --> 00:06:56,830
be accurate for long horizons.

142
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:59,270
And so you won't be able to
actually get very good state

143
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:02,910
coverage if you only start at
the initial state distribution

144
00:07:02,910 --> 00:07:07,910
and don't actually end,
because you only want

145
00:07:07,910 --> 00:07:09,920
to use fairly short rollouts.

146
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:11,670
You can also generate
partial trajectories

147
00:07:11,670 --> 00:07:13,033
from the initial states.

148
00:07:13,033 --> 00:07:15,450
But this won't give you good
coverage of the later states.

149
00:07:15,450 --> 00:07:20,830
And so one thing that gets
the best of both worlds-- it

150
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:23,550
gets you good coverage
and also allows

151
00:07:23,550 --> 00:07:25,350
you to use short
trajectories-- is

152
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:28,110
to generate these
partial trajectories

153
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:32,130
from all of the states
in your real data set.

154
00:07:32,130 --> 00:07:34,950

155
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:39,230
So then what the full
algorithm looks like is,

156
00:07:39,230 --> 00:07:43,660
you will first
collect real data.

157
00:07:43,660 --> 00:07:47,910

158
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:51,580
We can call this Denv, before.

159
00:07:51,580 --> 00:07:59,140
And then we will want to update
our model using the real data

160
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:02,360
so that our model is as
accurate as it can be.

161
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:06,660

162
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:12,280
And then we can generate data.

163
00:08:12,280 --> 00:08:20,260
And the way this generation
process will look like

164
00:08:20,260 --> 00:08:30,660
is first sample some
S from your real data.

165
00:08:30,660 --> 00:08:38,260
And then generate a short
trajectory starting from S,

166
00:08:38,260 --> 00:08:46,220
where you will roll out your
policy or whatever exploration

167
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:54,730
policy you have,
starting from S.

168
00:08:54,730 --> 00:08:57,290
But then we'll be
doing this rollout

169
00:08:57,290 --> 00:09:00,360
inside your learned model.

170
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:05,410

171
00:09:05,410 --> 00:09:09,290
And so this will be
inside the world that

172
00:09:09,290 --> 00:09:14,050
is instantiated from your
learned model and not

173
00:09:14,050 --> 00:09:15,730
in the real environment.

174
00:09:15,730 --> 00:09:22,130
This data will go into
some other data set.

175
00:09:22,130 --> 00:09:28,570
Maybe we can call it Dgen
for D-generated data.

176
00:09:28,570 --> 00:09:32,570
And then at the
end, you can simply

177
00:09:32,570 --> 00:09:36,210
update your policy
using both the real data

178
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:38,810
and the generated data.

179
00:09:38,810 --> 00:09:53,450
So update pi theta using
Denv and D generated.

180
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:56,170
And then repeat this process.

181
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:56,970
Whoops.

182
00:09:56,970 --> 00:09:58,020
I misspelled real.

183
00:09:58,020 --> 00:10:02,993

184
00:10:02,993 --> 00:10:04,410
So the question
is, if you're only

185
00:10:04,410 --> 00:10:07,530
generating short trajectories,
does that take longer

186
00:10:07,530 --> 00:10:11,170
to converge to some goal?

187
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:20,370
So you can think of it
as kind of supplementing

188
00:10:20,370 --> 00:10:22,030
the data that you have so far.

189
00:10:22,030 --> 00:10:28,650
And when you start from states
that are closer to the goal

190
00:10:28,650 --> 00:10:32,710
from when you
generate data there,

191
00:10:32,710 --> 00:10:34,690
then you'll be close
to getting rewards.

192
00:10:34,690 --> 00:10:36,810
And you'll be able to update.

193
00:10:36,810 --> 00:10:40,250
When you're further,
this doesn't mean that--

194
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:42,150
this data won't
necessarily reach the goal.

195
00:10:42,150 --> 00:10:44,830
But with reinforcement
learning algorithms,

196
00:10:44,830 --> 00:10:49,000
you'll be able to
understand-- some of this data

197
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,840
might feed into states
that are in the data.

198
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:55,000
And when you use
reinforcement learning,

199
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:56,500
you can then learn that--

200
00:10:56,500 --> 00:10:59,360
like, as an example, maybe
you have a real trajectory

201
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:03,560
that takes a path that is
maybe not the most direct path.

202
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:06,200
And then you start learning
a policy that knows,

203
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:08,620
maybe I should shortcut this.

204
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:10,280
Then your model,
when you explore

205
00:11:10,280 --> 00:11:13,160
that in your
synthetic data, this

206
00:11:13,160 --> 00:11:15,560
might actually help you
learn a better policy

207
00:11:15,560 --> 00:11:18,120
by using this kind of data.

208
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:21,200
In terms of convergence
speed or speed

209
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,560
in which you'll
get a good policy,

210
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:32,960
generally, the thing that
we're often trying to minimize

211
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:36,340
is the amount of real data
that we need right here.

212
00:11:36,340 --> 00:11:40,200
And this will allow you to get a
good policy with less real data

213
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,980
by using these red trajectories.

214
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:43,480
Yeah.

215
00:11:43,480 --> 00:11:45,772
So the question is, how do
we get rewards in this case?

216
00:11:45,772 --> 00:11:49,030
So I haven't
explicitly written it

217
00:11:49,030 --> 00:11:52,950
out here because
sometimes you might

218
00:11:52,950 --> 00:11:54,740
know the form of
the reward function.

219
00:11:54,740 --> 00:11:58,230

220
00:11:58,230 --> 00:12:01,150
Or you might have
learned a reward function

221
00:12:01,150 --> 00:12:03,910
in the original RL problem, in
which case you can use that.

222
00:12:03,910 --> 00:12:07,350

223
00:12:07,350 --> 00:12:10,375
If you didn't learn
a reward function

224
00:12:10,375 --> 00:12:11,750
and the real world
just gives you

225
00:12:11,750 --> 00:12:13,890
the rewards as
part of this data,

226
00:12:13,890 --> 00:12:16,670
then when you train
this model, you'll

227
00:12:16,670 --> 00:12:20,030
need for either this
model or a separate model

228
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:24,190
to also learn a reward function
with supervised learning.

229
00:12:24,190 --> 00:12:26,270
So the question
was about compute.

230
00:12:26,270 --> 00:12:29,710
So this is going to be
more compute intensive

231
00:12:29,710 --> 00:12:33,790
during training than if you
were to not use this, especially

232
00:12:33,790 --> 00:12:37,030
because sometimes the models
that you might be learning--

233
00:12:37,030 --> 00:12:40,170
in some cases, the models
that you're learning,

234
00:12:40,170 --> 00:12:43,950
usually rolling out through
them, takes compute.

235
00:12:43,950 --> 00:12:48,020
You're kind of trading data in
the environment for compute.

236
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:50,780
And one of the reasons why that
trade-off is often favorable

237
00:12:50,780 --> 00:12:53,740
is because collecting online
data in the real environment

238
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,940
might be unsafe,
or it might just

239
00:12:55,940 --> 00:12:58,580
be really expensive if you
have to run experiments

240
00:12:58,580 --> 00:13:02,140
on physical equipment.

241
00:13:02,140 --> 00:13:05,260
Unlike planning, however, this
is not more compute intensive

242
00:13:05,260 --> 00:13:06,195
at test time.

243
00:13:06,195 --> 00:13:07,820
Because you're using
the synthetic data

244
00:13:07,820 --> 00:13:09,320
to update a policy.

245
00:13:09,320 --> 00:13:12,220
And then at test time,
you're running that policy

246
00:13:12,220 --> 00:13:13,790
as your final behavior.

247
00:13:13,790 --> 00:13:18,340

248
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:19,700
Cool.

249
00:13:19,700 --> 00:13:23,740
So the algorithm on the
slides, we collect data.

250
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:24,920
We update our model.

251
00:13:24,920 --> 00:13:27,580
This model may
also model rewards

252
00:13:27,580 --> 00:13:30,660
by trying to mimic the
data from the environment.

253
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:37,700
Then we will collect these short
rollouts by using our model,

254
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:41,180
starting from states
in the real data.

255
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:43,740
And then we'll update the
policy and, optionally,

256
00:13:43,740 --> 00:13:47,540
also update any sort
of value function using

257
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:51,980
the aggregated data.

258
00:13:51,980 --> 00:13:56,020
One other thing
to note about this

259
00:13:56,020 --> 00:14:00,060
is that it is compatible with
a variety of reinforcement

260
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:01,080
learning algorithms.

261
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:06,580
So step 4, you can use a
variety of algorithms here.

262
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:09,700
You can even use
on-policy reinforcement

263
00:14:09,700 --> 00:14:11,980
learning algorithms as well.

264
00:14:11,980 --> 00:14:15,780
In particular, if you have
an on-policy algorithm that

265
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:18,700
needs data from
the exact pi theta

266
00:14:18,700 --> 00:14:21,140
that you're taking
a gradient step on,

267
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:22,740
all that means is
that pi theta has

268
00:14:22,740 --> 00:14:26,020
to be the policy that was
collecting the synthetic data

269
00:14:26,020 --> 00:14:26,770
through the model.

270
00:14:26,770 --> 00:14:31,780

271
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:32,580
Cool.

272
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:33,080
Yeah.

273
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:37,340
So with regard to this
last update, here,

274
00:14:37,340 --> 00:14:39,820
we're using both the data from
the environment and the data

275
00:14:39,820 --> 00:14:41,020
from the model.

276
00:14:41,020 --> 00:14:43,770
There are actually also
some policy updates

277
00:14:43,770 --> 00:14:45,890
that only use data
from the model

278
00:14:45,890 --> 00:14:51,510
and find that that
works fine as well.

279
00:14:51,510 --> 00:14:53,250
So you can choose
to use either both

280
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:56,430
or just use the
data from the model.

281
00:14:56,430 --> 00:14:56,930
Yeah.

282
00:14:56,930 --> 00:14:59,210
So this only makes sense
when you have an estimate

283
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:00,450
of the reward function.

284
00:15:00,450 --> 00:15:02,370
And if you don't,
then you won't be

285
00:15:02,370 --> 00:15:06,463
able to update your policy
on the generated data.

286
00:15:06,463 --> 00:15:08,130
So the question-- is
the learning stable

287
00:15:08,130 --> 00:15:09,520
when you use the synthetic data?

288
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:13,730

289
00:15:13,730 --> 00:15:15,970
Yeah, yeah.

290
00:15:15,970 --> 00:15:18,370
So you may be in a
scenario where you're

291
00:15:18,370 --> 00:15:19,830
adding a lot of synthetic data.

292
00:15:19,830 --> 00:15:22,330
And so how much synthetic
data you generate

293
00:15:22,330 --> 00:15:26,770
and how you balance that
data in this last update

294
00:15:26,770 --> 00:15:29,810
might depend on how good
you think your model is.

295
00:15:29,810 --> 00:15:35,450
In terms of stability,
I think that--

296
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:38,330
we'll talk about pros and
cons on the next slide.

297
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:40,510
But one downside of
these kinds of algorithms

298
00:15:40,510 --> 00:15:44,280
is, they do introduce additional
hyperparameters because you

299
00:15:44,280 --> 00:15:45,340
have to train this model.

300
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:48,780
And if, for example, your model
training, for whatever reason,

301
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:52,880
it didn't do well, then you
might end up in a scenario

302
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:56,800
where the algorithm
performs more

303
00:15:56,800 --> 00:15:59,000
poorly because those
hyperparameters weren't

304
00:15:59,000 --> 00:15:59,900
well tuned.

305
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:02,880
And so you can see issues there.

306
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:13,120
But otherwise, if you have
a fairly accurate model,

307
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:18,400
you usually don't have issues
otherwise with stability.

308
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,800
So let's talk a
little bit about when

309
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:23,500
to use this sort
of synthetic data

310
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:25,820
and when to use model-based
reinforcement learning.

311
00:16:25,820 --> 00:16:28,720
So generally, the
way to think about it

312
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:31,320
is that there's
potentially really big

313
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:33,400
upsides with model-based
reinforcement learning

314
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:36,800
and also potentially
big downsides.

315
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:42,030
The big upside is that if you
can learn a pretty good model,

316
00:16:42,030 --> 00:16:46,710
then it is immensely useful to
be able to predict the future.

317
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:51,230
And you can get away with
using far, far less real data

318
00:16:51,230 --> 00:16:55,190
by using the data
that's generated.

319
00:16:55,190 --> 00:16:59,630
And so in domains where it's
feasible to learn a model,

320
00:16:59,630 --> 00:17:00,830
it's really, really useful.

321
00:17:00,830 --> 00:17:03,870
You could also do the kind of
test time planning as well.

322
00:17:03,870 --> 00:17:07,052
And generally, maybe
one other point

323
00:17:07,052 --> 00:17:09,010
that's not on the slides
with regard to upsides

324
00:17:09,010 --> 00:17:11,150
is that it seems like
this notion of being

325
00:17:11,150 --> 00:17:15,240
able to anticipate future states
as an outcome of your actions,

326
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:16,990
that seems like a
fairly fundamental thing

327
00:17:16,990 --> 00:17:20,930
that our models should at least
have some understanding for.

328
00:17:20,930 --> 00:17:23,187

329
00:17:23,187 --> 00:17:25,270
One other thing that we
actually didn't talk about

330
00:17:25,270 --> 00:17:28,550
is, there are also scenarios
where the model can be

331
00:17:28,550 --> 00:17:30,430
trained without reward labels.

332
00:17:30,430 --> 00:17:35,350
And, for example, if some of
your real data has rewards

333
00:17:35,350 --> 00:17:37,510
and some of it
doesn't have rewards,

334
00:17:37,510 --> 00:17:39,130
the part that
doesn't have rewards,

335
00:17:39,130 --> 00:17:41,670
you can train your model on.

336
00:17:41,670 --> 00:17:44,230
And your model can actually
leverage potentially more data

337
00:17:44,230 --> 00:17:46,790
than what a model-free
reinforcement learning

338
00:17:46,790 --> 00:17:49,830
method can use.

339
00:17:49,830 --> 00:17:51,735
And so that's quite nice.

340
00:17:51,735 --> 00:17:53,110
And there are
scenarios where you

341
00:17:53,110 --> 00:17:55,235
might be able to collect
very large amounts of data

342
00:17:55,235 --> 00:17:57,580
without rewards and a small
amount of data with rewards.

343
00:17:57,580 --> 00:18:00,550

344
00:18:00,550 --> 00:18:03,070
And then the other thing is
that the model is somewhat

345
00:18:03,070 --> 00:18:03,970
task agnostic.

346
00:18:03,970 --> 00:18:06,690
And so if it was trained on a
broad enough data distribution,

347
00:18:06,690 --> 00:18:09,870
sometimes you can transfer this
model across different tasks

348
00:18:09,870 --> 00:18:11,870
or across different rewards.

349
00:18:11,870 --> 00:18:15,390
And so, for example,
if your goal isn't just

350
00:18:15,390 --> 00:18:17,990
to learn one task but to
learn many different tasks,

351
00:18:17,990 --> 00:18:20,860
this sort of
transferability is useful.

352
00:18:20,860 --> 00:18:23,470

353
00:18:23,470 --> 00:18:24,730
So those are the big upsides.

354
00:18:24,730 --> 00:18:27,790
What about the big downsides?

355
00:18:27,790 --> 00:18:30,710
One downside is that the model
doesn't optimize for task

356
00:18:30,710 --> 00:18:34,230
performance and errors.

357
00:18:34,230 --> 00:18:36,250
Because it's trying
to-- for example,

358
00:18:36,250 --> 00:18:39,640
if you have image observations
or your states are images,

359
00:18:39,640 --> 00:18:43,660
then it's trying to get the
details in the pixels to line up

360
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:47,980
and that, for example, the color
of the sky is the correct color.

361
00:18:47,980 --> 00:18:50,300
And that, in some
ways, oftentimes

362
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:53,220
is not actually what
you care about in terms

363
00:18:53,220 --> 00:18:54,940
of learning a good policy.

364
00:18:54,940 --> 00:18:57,020
So there's this
mismatch between what

365
00:18:57,020 --> 00:18:58,860
you're optimizing for
when you train a model

366
00:18:58,860 --> 00:19:01,020
and what you actually
might care about,

367
00:19:01,020 --> 00:19:03,120
in terms of downstream
policy performance.

368
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:05,780

369
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,960
Sometimes it's really hard
to learn a good model.

370
00:19:07,960 --> 00:19:09,752
Sometimes it's harder
to learn a good model

371
00:19:09,752 --> 00:19:11,300
than it is to learn
a good policy.

372
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:13,300
In these cases,
usually, you don't

373
00:19:13,300 --> 00:19:18,660
want to learn models because
you're then presenting a harder

374
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:21,780
problem than the actual
downstream problem you

375
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:24,197
might care about.

376
00:19:24,197 --> 00:19:25,780
And then the last
thing that I alluded

377
00:19:25,780 --> 00:19:28,360
to before is that it's another
thing you have to train.

378
00:19:28,360 --> 00:19:30,060
And so there's more
hyperparameters,

379
00:19:30,060 --> 00:19:32,300
and it's also more
compute intensive.

380
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:35,810
And so because of these
big upsides and downsides,

381
00:19:35,810 --> 00:19:39,010
generally, whether
to try to learn

382
00:19:39,010 --> 00:19:42,890
this kind of predictive model
depends a lot on the domain

383
00:19:42,890 --> 00:19:46,590
and how hard it is to
learn that kind of model.

384
00:19:46,590 --> 00:19:47,090
Yeah.

385
00:19:47,090 --> 00:19:48,923
So the question was,
what are a few examples

386
00:19:48,923 --> 00:19:50,770
of the additional
hyperparameters?

387
00:19:50,770 --> 00:19:55,170
The main thing all comes down
to this step 2 right here,

388
00:19:55,170 --> 00:19:59,010
which is that you need to design
the architecture of the model,

389
00:19:59,010 --> 00:20:00,453
of this predictive model.

390
00:20:00,453 --> 00:20:02,370
And it might need to be
a completely different

391
00:20:02,370 --> 00:20:03,953
architecture from
the policy if you're

392
00:20:03,953 --> 00:20:06,890
going to be generating
images or states.

393
00:20:06,890 --> 00:20:10,197
The second is that you
need a learning rate.

394
00:20:10,197 --> 00:20:12,030
You need to know how
long to train that for,

395
00:20:12,030 --> 00:20:17,530
like how many epochs, any
regularization for that model,

396
00:20:17,530 --> 00:20:20,410
basically everything that
might go into training

397
00:20:20,410 --> 00:20:22,330
a model like this.

398
00:20:22,330 --> 00:20:24,872
One more thing on that
previous question--

399
00:20:24,872 --> 00:20:26,330
beyond training
this model, there's

400
00:20:26,330 --> 00:20:29,730
also hyperparameters with regard
to the length of these rollouts

401
00:20:29,730 --> 00:20:33,090
and how much synthetic
data to generate.

402
00:20:33,090 --> 00:20:36,330
So the question is, do we
keep on collecting data

403
00:20:36,330 --> 00:20:37,830
after each policy update?

404
00:20:37,830 --> 00:20:43,670
So as written, this is
an online algorithm.

405
00:20:43,670 --> 00:20:47,410
You can actually also develop
completely offline versions,

406
00:20:47,410 --> 00:20:49,170
model-based RL
versions as well, where

407
00:20:49,170 --> 00:20:52,610
you have a fixed prior
data set and you augment

408
00:20:52,610 --> 00:20:54,270
that data set with a model.

409
00:20:54,270 --> 00:20:57,342
And then you train a
policy, and you're done.

410
00:20:57,342 --> 00:20:59,410
In this case, we have
an online algorithm,

411
00:20:59,410 --> 00:21:02,190
where we then continue
to collect real data,

412
00:21:02,190 --> 00:21:04,240
update our model, and so forth.

413
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:07,230

414
00:21:07,230 --> 00:21:09,090
This can be done in
an off-policy way

415
00:21:09,090 --> 00:21:10,370
or an on-policy way.

416
00:21:10,370 --> 00:21:14,150
So that's going to come down
to, when you do this update,

417
00:21:14,150 --> 00:21:19,250
is it going to only use the Denv
and the Dgen from this iteration

418
00:21:19,250 --> 00:21:21,890
or also from
previous iterations?

419
00:21:21,890 --> 00:21:24,490
And that's going to
depend on what algorithm

420
00:21:24,490 --> 00:21:26,805
you use to update your policy.

421
00:21:26,805 --> 00:21:28,430
So generally, models
are very flexible.

422
00:21:28,430 --> 00:21:31,450
You can use them
offline, on policy,

423
00:21:31,450 --> 00:21:33,300
off policy, and so forth.

424
00:21:33,300 --> 00:21:33,800
Yeah.

425
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:37,280
So the question
is, how hard is it

426
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:40,200
to learn a model
compared to a policy?

427
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:41,980
It's extremely domain dependent.

428
00:21:41,980 --> 00:21:48,960
So for example, say
you're in a maze.

429
00:21:48,960 --> 00:21:53,980
So you're trying to learn
how to get from here to here.

430
00:21:53,980 --> 00:21:56,000
Maybe there's some walls.

431
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:00,520
And you need to
figure out how to get

432
00:22:00,520 --> 00:22:04,000
through this sort
of environment.

433
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,080
And you're not able to
go through the walls.

434
00:22:06,080 --> 00:22:09,180
In this case, the model
might be quite easy to learn.

435
00:22:09,180 --> 00:22:11,777
You'll learn that if your
action is to go right,

436
00:22:11,777 --> 00:22:13,860
then you'll go right,
unless there's a wall there.

437
00:22:13,860 --> 00:22:16,920
If you're going to go up and
so forth-- that's really easy.

438
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:18,800
But the policy is
really complicated.

439
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:21,720
Because the policy is saying
that, OK, if you're here,

440
00:22:21,720 --> 00:22:24,480
maybe you need to go in
various different directions.

441
00:22:24,480 --> 00:22:28,840
And so this is a case where the
policy is a lot more complicated

442
00:22:28,840 --> 00:22:29,820
than the model.

443
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:32,630
And learning a model
could be a good choice.

444
00:22:32,630 --> 00:22:37,110
As another example,
say that you're

445
00:22:37,110 --> 00:22:40,790
trying to pour some
water from one cup

446
00:22:40,790 --> 00:22:44,070
into a bowl or
something like that.

447
00:22:44,070 --> 00:22:46,610
The policy might be
potentially fairly simple.

448
00:22:46,610 --> 00:22:52,510
Like, maybe you're already even
holding the cup in your hand.

449
00:22:52,510 --> 00:22:54,430
Then the policy might
be as simple as just

450
00:22:54,430 --> 00:22:56,750
rotating your wrist.

451
00:22:56,750 --> 00:22:58,710
But the model in
this sort of case

452
00:22:58,710 --> 00:23:02,710
might involve the fluid dynamics
of how the water will pour

453
00:23:02,710 --> 00:23:05,430
and what will happen,
for example, if it hits

454
00:23:05,430 --> 00:23:07,590
the edge of the bowl or
the center of the bowl

455
00:23:07,590 --> 00:23:08,850
or something like that.

456
00:23:08,850 --> 00:23:12,230
And this is a scenario where the
model is a lot more complicated

457
00:23:12,230 --> 00:23:13,750
than the policy.

458
00:23:13,750 --> 00:23:17,828
And it's possible
that in this scenario,

459
00:23:17,828 --> 00:23:19,870
it might be a lot easier
to just learn the policy

460
00:23:19,870 --> 00:23:21,270
without learning the model.

461
00:23:21,270 --> 00:23:23,210
So it really depends
on the domain.

462
00:23:23,210 --> 00:23:23,710
Yeah.

463
00:23:23,710 --> 00:23:26,150
So the question is, how
successful has model-based RL

464
00:23:26,150 --> 00:23:28,530
been in robotics scenarios?

465
00:23:28,530 --> 00:23:32,300

466
00:23:32,300 --> 00:23:34,340
If you're dealing with
high-dimensional image

467
00:23:34,340 --> 00:23:38,060
observations, it's arguably
been less successful.

468
00:23:38,060 --> 00:23:41,420
If you have a more
compact state space,

469
00:23:41,420 --> 00:23:44,480
like the positions of joints
and positions of objects,

470
00:23:44,480 --> 00:23:46,940
then it's been a
lot more successful.

471
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:51,960
And with legged
robots, for example,

472
00:23:51,960 --> 00:23:56,600
you can use model-based
RL pretty successfully

473
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:59,100
if you're mostly just trying
to learn, like, a blind walking

474
00:23:59,100 --> 00:24:00,180
policy.

475
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:03,620
Or we saw the dexterous--
like, the hand manipulation,

476
00:24:03,620 --> 00:24:06,460
where you knew the
position of the two balls.

477
00:24:06,460 --> 00:24:08,480
It was also very
successful for that.

478
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:12,500
And I haven't tried to do
model-free RL for that problem.

479
00:24:12,500 --> 00:24:15,707
Or even imitation learning
would be very, very challenging.

480
00:24:15,707 --> 00:24:17,540
And so it's been extremely
successful there.

481
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:20,220
In scenarios where you need
to have image observations,

482
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:21,620
it gets a lot harder.

483
00:24:21,620 --> 00:24:24,238
It doesn't mean that--

484
00:24:24,238 --> 00:24:26,280
video-generative models
are getting a lot better.

485
00:24:26,280 --> 00:24:31,660
And so I could see a future
where this sort of approach

486
00:24:31,660 --> 00:24:35,960
might also be quite
successful in the future.

487
00:24:35,960 --> 00:24:38,720
But right now, it hasn't
been super successful.

488
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:41,300

489
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:43,420
The last thing I'll
mention on model-based RL

490
00:24:43,420 --> 00:24:47,660
is that so far, we've been
modeling this predictive model

491
00:24:47,660 --> 00:24:49,140
of the future.

492
00:24:49,140 --> 00:24:51,020
And there's actually
a lot of other objects

493
00:24:51,020 --> 00:24:52,700
that you could learn.

494
00:24:52,700 --> 00:24:56,400
So you could learn what's
called an inverse model.

495
00:24:56,400 --> 00:24:58,690
You could learn,
for two states--

496
00:24:58,690 --> 00:25:01,430

497
00:25:01,430 --> 00:25:04,400
like, if I'm
currently in state st,

498
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:08,290
what is the action that would
lead to state st plus 1?

499
00:25:08,290 --> 00:25:10,040
You could learn a
multistep inverse model,

500
00:25:10,040 --> 00:25:15,420
which is, if I'm going to
end up at state st plus n,

501
00:25:15,420 --> 00:25:16,800
what action would lead me there?

502
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,258
Or what are the
sequence of actions

503
00:25:18,258 --> 00:25:20,860
that would lead me there?

504
00:25:20,860 --> 00:25:23,500
Both of these, in some ways,
look closer to a policy,

505
00:25:23,500 --> 00:25:28,570
except you're passing as input
some future state rather than

506
00:25:28,570 --> 00:25:30,570
just the current state.

507
00:25:30,570 --> 00:25:32,450
These are also things
that can be learned

508
00:25:32,450 --> 00:25:34,313
without data with rewards.

509
00:25:34,313 --> 00:25:36,730
You're kind of just learning
about the environment and not

510
00:25:36,730 --> 00:25:40,410
about the particular
task at hand.

511
00:25:40,410 --> 00:25:42,390
You can predict futures
without actions.

512
00:25:42,390 --> 00:25:45,210
And that can think about,
what are the affordances

513
00:25:45,210 --> 00:25:46,070
in my environment?

514
00:25:46,070 --> 00:25:48,650
What are the things
that could be

515
00:25:48,650 --> 00:25:50,270
possible to happen
in the future,

516
00:25:50,270 --> 00:25:53,250
irrespective of the
actions that I take?

517
00:25:53,250 --> 00:25:59,490
You could try to interpolate
between two states.

518
00:25:59,490 --> 00:26:03,902
You could learn a generative
model of transitions of st

519
00:26:03,902 --> 00:26:05,250
at st plus 1.

520
00:26:05,250 --> 00:26:10,050
There's some cool properties
with that sort of model.

521
00:26:10,050 --> 00:26:14,370
And these kind of models
can be pretty useful too.

522
00:26:14,370 --> 00:26:16,232
They have various purposes.

523
00:26:16,232 --> 00:26:18,690
There's lots of research on
using different kinds of models

524
00:26:18,690 --> 00:26:19,970
like these.

525
00:26:19,970 --> 00:26:21,690
And so it's just
something to think

526
00:26:21,690 --> 00:26:24,370
about in terms of in some
domains you could imagine.

527
00:26:24,370 --> 00:26:27,960
Or with some data sources,
you could imagine scenarios

528
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:29,980
where these might be useful.

529
00:26:29,980 --> 00:26:30,480
Yeah.

530
00:26:30,480 --> 00:26:32,640
So the question
is, if you use data

531
00:26:32,640 --> 00:26:35,580
that doesn't have rewards as
part of your model training,

532
00:26:35,580 --> 00:26:37,800
is there going to be a
mismatch between the accuracy

533
00:26:37,800 --> 00:26:41,800
of predicting next states
and predicting rewards?

534
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:47,160
There certainly could
be a mismatch like that.

535
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:50,720
Generally, rewards are
a bit easier to learn

536
00:26:50,720 --> 00:26:53,240
how to predict
than future states.

537
00:26:53,240 --> 00:26:58,490
And so even if you
have a lot more data

538
00:26:58,490 --> 00:27:00,240
for learning the model,
you could actually

539
00:27:00,240 --> 00:27:01,740
end up in a scenario
where the model

540
00:27:01,740 --> 00:27:05,237
is less accurate than
the estimated reward.

541
00:27:05,237 --> 00:27:07,320
But yeah, you can definitely
end up with scenarios

542
00:27:07,320 --> 00:27:09,040
where the reward
is less accurate

543
00:27:09,040 --> 00:27:12,000
and where you need to
specifically gather

544
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,180
data for that.

545
00:27:14,180 --> 00:27:14,680
Yeah.

546
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:17,400

547
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:17,900
Cool.

548
00:27:17,900 --> 00:27:18,400
OK.

549
00:27:18,400 --> 00:27:21,680
So that's it for model-based RL.

550
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:25,710
Now we're going to talk a
bit about multitask learning

551
00:27:25,710 --> 00:27:28,710
and what that looks like and
also different techniques

552
00:27:28,710 --> 00:27:33,710
that we can do, beyond a fairly
naive and simple technique.

553
00:27:33,710 --> 00:27:38,790
So what is multitask
reinforcement learning?

554
00:27:38,790 --> 00:27:44,190
The key idea is that we want to
be able to learn a generalist

555
00:27:44,190 --> 00:27:47,830
policy that isn't just
aiming to do one thing,

556
00:27:47,830 --> 00:27:52,270
like being able to pour
water, for example,

557
00:27:52,270 --> 00:27:56,350
but that can do many
different things.

558
00:27:56,350 --> 00:27:58,510
The examples of this--

559
00:27:58,510 --> 00:28:01,190
we might want a generalist
language model assistant

560
00:28:01,190 --> 00:28:02,950
that can book
travel for us, that

561
00:28:02,950 --> 00:28:05,830
can do online grocery shopping.

562
00:28:05,830 --> 00:28:08,490
We might want a legged robot
that can walk, that can run,

563
00:28:08,490 --> 00:28:12,110
that can dance, that can crouch,
maybe a mobile robot that

564
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:16,550
can hang up a towel, unload
a dishwasher, wipe a spill,

565
00:28:16,550 --> 00:28:19,710
maybe want a music recommender
system that can personalize

566
00:28:19,710 --> 00:28:23,270
recommendations for
one person and also

567
00:28:23,270 --> 00:28:25,070
be able to personalize
recommendations

568
00:28:25,070 --> 00:28:27,350
to a different person.

569
00:28:27,350 --> 00:28:29,750
Or we might want
something that can

570
00:28:29,750 --> 00:28:31,550
play lots of different
games, like being

571
00:28:31,550 --> 00:28:35,790
able to play Pokemon and pinball
and Flappy Bird and so forth.

572
00:28:35,790 --> 00:28:38,070
And there are
scenarios where if you

573
00:28:38,070 --> 00:28:41,590
think about each of these tasks
as their own reinforcement

574
00:28:41,590 --> 00:28:44,550
learning problem, they
might have different reward

575
00:28:44,550 --> 00:28:46,510
functions from other tasks.

576
00:28:46,510 --> 00:28:49,410
They might, in some cases,
have different dynamics,

577
00:28:49,410 --> 00:28:51,750
like the dynamics
of different games

578
00:28:51,750 --> 00:28:54,030
or the dynamics of
different people.

579
00:28:54,030 --> 00:28:58,390
They might have different
action spaces, even.

580
00:28:58,390 --> 00:29:02,510
And so when you're
trying to train policies

581
00:29:02,510 --> 00:29:04,270
across these different
tasks, there's

582
00:29:04,270 --> 00:29:09,210
some considerations that come
up when you try to do that.

583
00:29:09,210 --> 00:29:11,830

584
00:29:11,830 --> 00:29:16,370
Now, the key idea that we'll see
is that you can, in many cases,

585
00:29:16,370 --> 00:29:18,110
just go about
normal reinforcement

586
00:29:18,110 --> 00:29:20,110
learning but
condition on the task

587
00:29:20,110 --> 00:29:21,830
that you're trying to learn.

588
00:29:21,830 --> 00:29:27,100
So you're then going to be
learning a policy that is not

589
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:29,540
just conditioned on
the state, but It's

590
00:29:29,540 --> 00:29:31,928
conditioned on the
state and also the task

591
00:29:31,928 --> 00:29:33,970
that you want it to be
performing for that state.

592
00:29:33,970 --> 00:29:36,740

593
00:29:36,740 --> 00:29:39,660
Now, why do we care about
multitask reinforcement

594
00:29:39,660 --> 00:29:40,740
learning?

595
00:29:40,740 --> 00:29:44,878
So if we go back to
a lot of the topics

596
00:29:44,878 --> 00:29:46,420
that we've talked
about in the course

597
00:29:46,420 --> 00:29:50,100
so far, like imitation learning,
on-policy and off-policy RL,

598
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:53,060
offline RL, model-based
RL, reward functions,

599
00:29:53,060 --> 00:29:57,735
a big topic that's recurred
in many of these lectures

600
00:29:57,735 --> 00:29:59,110
is talking about
data efficiency.

601
00:29:59,110 --> 00:30:03,820
Like, how much data do we
need to be able to calculate

602
00:30:03,820 --> 00:30:07,140
a gradient that is accurate?

603
00:30:07,140 --> 00:30:11,620
And if our ultimate goal
is to train something

604
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:14,620
that can do multiple
tasks at once,

605
00:30:14,620 --> 00:30:16,980
then we can actually
amortize the complexity

606
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:20,100
of learning any one
task by actually

607
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:24,370
training that policy across
tasks and across scenarios.

608
00:30:24,370 --> 00:30:28,370
So it could actually be a boost
for data efficiency in the sense

609
00:30:28,370 --> 00:30:32,570
that the data efficiency
for training any one task

610
00:30:32,570 --> 00:30:35,010
will be lower when
you're actually trying

611
00:30:35,010 --> 00:30:37,050
to train everything together.

612
00:30:37,050 --> 00:30:40,910
And that's because
tasks typically

613
00:30:40,910 --> 00:30:43,410
will have some sort
of shared structure,

614
00:30:43,410 --> 00:30:46,947
if you're trying to be a
language model assistant,

615
00:30:46,947 --> 00:30:49,530
there is going to be a shared
structure with regard to grammar

616
00:30:49,530 --> 00:30:52,170
or maybe with regard
to different websites.

617
00:30:52,170 --> 00:30:54,327
And if you learn how to
interact with one website,

618
00:30:54,327 --> 00:30:56,410
that can teach you something
about how to interact

619
00:30:56,410 --> 00:30:58,272
with other websites.

620
00:30:58,272 --> 00:30:59,730
Or with legged
robots, if your goal

621
00:30:59,730 --> 00:31:02,290
is to be able to walk
forward and walk backward,

622
00:31:02,290 --> 00:31:06,210
then this shared notion
of staying balanced

623
00:31:06,210 --> 00:31:09,290
will be shared across
tasks, among other things.

624
00:31:09,290 --> 00:31:13,250
Or if you're trying to develop
a recommender system that's

625
00:31:13,250 --> 00:31:15,570
personalized to different
users, then some users

626
00:31:15,570 --> 00:31:17,142
will share a lot
of similarities,

627
00:31:17,142 --> 00:31:19,600
where if you try to learn to
be a great recommender for one

628
00:31:19,600 --> 00:31:23,560
person, then there are other
people that you could also

629
00:31:23,560 --> 00:31:27,040
probably be a recommender for.

630
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:30,200
And then there's also a slightly
deeper motivation as well,

631
00:31:30,200 --> 00:31:33,560
which is that when
we actually try

632
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:36,280
to change these general systems
rather than systems that

633
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:40,600
do one task, we often find
that generalist systems end up

634
00:31:40,600 --> 00:31:43,240
being more reliable and
more performant than if we

635
00:31:43,240 --> 00:31:46,840
were to just train on one task.

636
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:50,240
And so I think that this is
a pretty cool finding that

637
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:54,320
has empirically been true
in recent years in AI.

638
00:31:54,320 --> 00:31:57,160
And so it's extra motivation
for not just training

639
00:31:57,160 --> 00:32:01,880
something for one particular
task or problem or user.

640
00:32:01,880 --> 00:32:04,260
Let's maybe get into what
this actually entails.

641
00:32:04,260 --> 00:32:06,600
And we actually
need to think about,

642
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:09,640
how do we think
about what a task is?

643
00:32:09,640 --> 00:32:12,480
It's actually, in some
ways, not very well defined,

644
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:14,480
what makes something a
different task versus not

645
00:32:14,480 --> 00:32:15,920
a different task.

646
00:32:15,920 --> 00:32:19,480
But one way to think about it
is having different tasks just

647
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:22,560
be different RL problems,
different Markov decision

648
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:24,040
processes.

649
00:32:24,040 --> 00:32:28,960
And so each task will have
a state space, action space,

650
00:32:28,960 --> 00:32:33,960
an initial state distribution,
a transition model, and a reward

651
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:36,200
function.

652
00:32:36,200 --> 00:32:39,480
And I'm indexing
these with index i

653
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:42,340
to denote that this is the
state space for task i.

654
00:32:42,340 --> 00:32:45,120
This is the action
space for task i,

655
00:32:45,120 --> 00:32:47,740
the dynamics and reward
for task i, and so forth.

656
00:32:47,740 --> 00:32:51,680

657
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:54,600
Now, with this
definition, it's worth

658
00:32:54,600 --> 00:33:00,320
mentioning that when we think
of the notion of a task,

659
00:33:00,320 --> 00:33:04,120
there's a meaning under
the English word of task.

660
00:33:04,120 --> 00:33:07,440
And this notion of
a task being an MDP

661
00:33:07,440 --> 00:33:09,280
is actually potentially
quite different

662
00:33:09,280 --> 00:33:12,440
from that semantic
notion of a task,

663
00:33:12,440 --> 00:33:14,812
where you could actually
have different tasks that

664
00:33:14,812 --> 00:33:16,520
have potentially the
same reward function

665
00:33:16,520 --> 00:33:20,150
and only have
different dynamics.

666
00:33:20,150 --> 00:33:25,430
For example, if you're
trying to walk forward

667
00:33:25,430 --> 00:33:29,510
on grass versus trying to
walk forward on flat terrain,

668
00:33:29,510 --> 00:33:33,153
then that potentially could be
considered two different tasks

669
00:33:33,153 --> 00:33:35,070
under this framework,
even though they're both

670
00:33:35,070 --> 00:33:37,710
the task of walking forward.

671
00:33:37,710 --> 00:33:43,470
OK, so a few examples of
multitask problems that we might

672
00:33:43,470 --> 00:33:44,950
think about.

673
00:33:44,950 --> 00:33:48,470
One is this personalized
recommendation problem

674
00:33:48,470 --> 00:33:49,630
that I mentioned.

675
00:33:49,630 --> 00:33:53,190
In this case, different tasks
would be different users

676
00:33:53,190 --> 00:33:54,870
or different people.

677
00:33:54,870 --> 00:33:57,910
And different people might have
different reward functions.

678
00:33:57,910 --> 00:34:00,170
Like, if it's a
music recommendation,

679
00:34:00,170 --> 00:34:02,290
someone might really
love rock music.

680
00:34:02,290 --> 00:34:06,590
Another person really
might love hip-hop music.

681
00:34:06,590 --> 00:34:15,900
And also, the dynamics with
regard to how often they even

682
00:34:15,900 --> 00:34:19,090
listen to music and so forth
might also change across users.

683
00:34:19,090 --> 00:34:21,739

684
00:34:21,739 --> 00:34:24,940
Reinforcement learning-- this is
an application we haven't really

685
00:34:24,940 --> 00:34:28,219
talked about, but it's often
used in character animation

686
00:34:28,219 --> 00:34:31,139
in order to actually
create animations

687
00:34:31,139 --> 00:34:34,060
of different kinds
of characters,

688
00:34:34,060 --> 00:34:39,219
like people or other
animated characters that

689
00:34:39,219 --> 00:34:41,100
appear in movies.

690
00:34:41,100 --> 00:34:44,860
And you might want
to allow and be

691
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:47,659
able to animate a character to
do lots of different maneuvers.

692
00:34:47,659 --> 00:34:50,219
So you might want to be able
to have your character be

693
00:34:50,219 --> 00:34:52,139
able to do a backflip
or to be able to run

694
00:34:52,139 --> 00:34:54,060
or be able to do a cartwheel.

695
00:34:54,060 --> 00:34:58,020
And each of these would
be different tasks.

696
00:34:58,020 --> 00:35:00,420
Does anyone have
thoughts for these tasks?

697
00:35:00,420 --> 00:35:02,580
What is the thing that
would vary across tasks

698
00:35:02,580 --> 00:35:05,080
versus be fixed across tasks?

699
00:35:05,080 --> 00:35:05,580
Yeah.

700
00:35:05,580 --> 00:35:09,140
So in this scenario, the reward
will vary because in one case,

701
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:10,120
you want it to run.

702
00:35:10,120 --> 00:35:12,620
In another case, you want
it to do a cartwheel.

703
00:35:12,620 --> 00:35:17,540
And the dynamics or the
physics of the simulator

704
00:35:17,540 --> 00:35:21,020
will stay the same.

705
00:35:21,020 --> 00:35:21,900
Cool.

706
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:23,860
There's also been
works that's trying

707
00:35:23,860 --> 00:35:29,240
to do character animation
involving different garments,

708
00:35:29,240 --> 00:35:32,340
so like a character that can put
on a shirt versus a long sleeve

709
00:35:32,340 --> 00:35:33,925
shirt versus a dress.

710
00:35:33,925 --> 00:35:35,300
Does anyone have
thoughts on what

711
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:37,700
would vary across these tasks?

712
00:35:37,700 --> 00:35:38,200
Yeah.

713
00:35:38,200 --> 00:35:39,080
So the policy might vary.

714
00:35:39,080 --> 00:35:40,580
The policy will
also vary, actually,

715
00:35:40,580 --> 00:35:43,960
in all of these cases
for different tasks.

716
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:44,460
Yeah.

717
00:35:44,460 --> 00:35:47,980
So the physics of how
a long-sleeve shirt

718
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:51,660
versus a short-sleeve shirt will
behave will vary across tasks.

719
00:35:51,660 --> 00:35:54,340
I think I also put the initial
state distribution might also

720
00:35:54,340 --> 00:35:57,260
vary, whereas in
the first example,

721
00:35:57,260 --> 00:35:58,660
maybe the initial
state is always

722
00:35:58,660 --> 00:36:01,620
to, say, start standing
up, for example,

723
00:36:01,620 --> 00:36:05,620
whereas here, you have different
garments in the initial state.

724
00:36:05,620 --> 00:36:08,060
And then the last
example I put is, maybe

725
00:36:08,060 --> 00:36:11,020
different tasks are controlling
different robot arms.

726
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:14,170
And maybe one robot arm
looks like this with maybe 4

727
00:36:14,170 --> 00:36:15,093
degrees of freedom.

728
00:36:15,093 --> 00:36:16,510
This one has 6
degrees of freedom.

729
00:36:16,510 --> 00:36:18,850
This one has 7
degrees of freedom.

730
00:36:18,850 --> 00:36:21,010
Does anyone have
thoughts on what

731
00:36:21,010 --> 00:36:25,890
would vary across
tasks if one task is

732
00:36:25,890 --> 00:36:28,030
to control the first
robot to grasp an object,

733
00:36:28,030 --> 00:36:30,770
the second task is to control
the second robot to grasp

734
00:36:30,770 --> 00:36:32,510
an object, and so forth?

735
00:36:32,510 --> 00:36:33,010
Yeah.

736
00:36:33,010 --> 00:36:35,090
So the actions themselves--

737
00:36:35,090 --> 00:36:37,590
so you'll have different
dimensions of actions.

738
00:36:37,590 --> 00:36:38,090
Yeah.

739
00:36:38,090 --> 00:36:39,570
So the dynamics will
also be different.

740
00:36:39,570 --> 00:36:41,250
Because when you
move one joint, that

741
00:36:41,250 --> 00:36:44,150
will affect the other
joints differently.

742
00:36:44,150 --> 00:36:44,650
Yeah.

743
00:36:44,650 --> 00:36:46,670
So the states would
also vary as well.

744
00:36:46,670 --> 00:36:52,450
So how you represent
the arm, that

745
00:36:52,450 --> 00:36:54,170
will be a different dimension.

746
00:36:54,170 --> 00:36:57,950
What the actions even are will
also be different dimensional.

747
00:36:57,950 --> 00:37:00,710
And then the dynamics will
also vary quite a bit as well

748
00:37:00,710 --> 00:37:03,130
because how your
actions affect the robot

749
00:37:03,130 --> 00:37:06,690
arm are going to change.

750
00:37:06,690 --> 00:37:08,010
Cool.

751
00:37:08,010 --> 00:37:10,370
So those are examples
of different problems

752
00:37:10,370 --> 00:37:16,280
where we might want to be
training different tasks.

753
00:37:16,280 --> 00:37:19,040
The last thing that
we're missing here

754
00:37:19,040 --> 00:37:21,160
is, we need to figure
out how to identify

755
00:37:21,160 --> 00:37:24,080
one task from another
task so that we

756
00:37:24,080 --> 00:37:27,240
can tell our policy
what task it should

757
00:37:27,240 --> 00:37:32,760
be doing at a given point.

758
00:37:32,760 --> 00:37:35,540
And one way to do this is
to just assign an index.

759
00:37:35,540 --> 00:37:37,340
You could say this
robot is task 0.

760
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:38,460
This robot is task 1.

761
00:37:38,460 --> 00:37:42,800
This robot is task
2 and so forth.

762
00:37:42,800 --> 00:37:46,580
If you don't have any other
better way to identify a task,

763
00:37:46,580 --> 00:37:50,280
this is kind of
what you resort to.

764
00:37:50,280 --> 00:37:53,600
You could also have a language
description of the task.

765
00:37:53,600 --> 00:37:55,560
You could have a
video illustration

766
00:37:55,560 --> 00:37:57,145
of what the task is.

767
00:37:57,145 --> 00:37:58,520
There's actually
a number of ways

768
00:37:58,520 --> 00:38:02,800
to be creative with how you
describe the task to the policy.

769
00:38:02,800 --> 00:38:07,340
And we'll refer to each of these
as different task identifiers.

770
00:38:07,340 --> 00:38:12,310
So the task identifier
for the task of--

771
00:38:12,310 --> 00:38:17,950
well, actually, we'll see some
examples on the next slide.

772
00:38:17,950 --> 00:38:20,430
Now, once you have
the task identifier,

773
00:38:20,430 --> 00:38:23,870
an alternative view on
the multitask RL problem

774
00:38:23,870 --> 00:38:27,270
is simply just having
the task identifier

775
00:38:27,270 --> 00:38:30,030
be part of the state,
where we're writing

776
00:38:30,030 --> 00:38:32,830
s bar as the original state.

777
00:38:32,830 --> 00:38:35,430
And what this means
is that before,

778
00:38:35,430 --> 00:38:40,710
we had a kind of a
collection of MDPs.

779
00:38:40,710 --> 00:38:43,190
That's what's
written at the top.

780
00:38:43,190 --> 00:38:54,430
And we can construct a
single MDP that actually

781
00:38:54,430 --> 00:38:56,260
covers all of the tasks.

782
00:38:56,260 --> 00:39:00,910

783
00:39:00,910 --> 00:39:07,510
We have, for example, the
Pi of the initial state,

784
00:39:07,510 --> 00:39:15,230
Pi of s prime, given
s, a and ri of s, a.

785
00:39:15,230 --> 00:39:20,590
And this is like the dynamics
and the reward for each task.

786
00:39:20,590 --> 00:39:24,630
And we can think of
it as like, our goal

787
00:39:24,630 --> 00:39:28,870
is to solve all of the
MDPs for each of the tasks.

788
00:39:28,870 --> 00:39:33,110
But we can also think about
it as just a single MDP, where

789
00:39:33,110 --> 00:39:39,310
the state is now defined
as like, maybe this

790
00:39:39,310 --> 00:39:45,310
is the original state
space of these tasks.

791
00:39:45,310 --> 00:39:48,750
And the state is now defined
as the original state

792
00:39:48,750 --> 00:39:55,630
space and the task
identifier for that task.

793
00:39:55,630 --> 00:39:58,390
And then the reward
function, for example,

794
00:39:58,390 --> 00:40:00,870
in this modified MDP--

795
00:40:00,870 --> 00:40:03,190
that's just a single
MDP for all the tasks--

796
00:40:03,190 --> 00:40:13,580
is then going to be equal to--
let's say, just to be explicit,

797
00:40:13,580 --> 00:40:21,180
the reward for this
state and this action

798
00:40:21,180 --> 00:40:29,260
would then be equal
to ri of s bar a.

799
00:40:29,260 --> 00:40:33,140
And similarly, you can write
something out for the dynamics

800
00:40:33,140 --> 00:40:44,900
as well, where the aggregate
MDP for a given task,

801
00:40:44,900 --> 00:40:55,020
s prime given s bar zi comma a
is equal to the kind of dynamics

802
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:55,790
for that task.

803
00:40:55,790 --> 00:40:58,675

804
00:40:58,675 --> 00:41:00,300
This gets a little
bit more complicated

805
00:41:00,300 --> 00:41:04,260
if your tasks have different
state spaces and action spaces.

806
00:41:04,260 --> 00:41:07,310
And so if they
have, for example,

807
00:41:07,310 --> 00:41:08,970
different
dimensionalities, then you

808
00:41:08,970 --> 00:41:11,650
need to think about how you
would represent this state

809
00:41:11,650 --> 00:41:13,730
space in the MDP.

810
00:41:13,730 --> 00:41:16,010
If they have the same state
space and action space,

811
00:41:16,010 --> 00:41:19,410
you can basically construct
this aggregated Markov decision

812
00:41:19,410 --> 00:41:24,050
process with these
equations here.

813
00:41:24,050 --> 00:41:32,107
Now, if we go back to what we
think of as the single task

814
00:41:32,107 --> 00:41:34,690
reinforcement learning setting
that we've talked about so far,

815
00:41:34,690 --> 00:41:37,570
we can then think
about multitask RL

816
00:41:37,570 --> 00:41:41,427
but where the task identifier
is part of the state, where

817
00:41:41,427 --> 00:41:43,010
the task identifier
could be the task,

818
00:41:43,010 --> 00:41:45,850
like an index, a
language description.

819
00:41:45,850 --> 00:41:48,010
It could also be some
desired goal state

820
00:41:48,010 --> 00:41:50,570
as well that you want to reach.

821
00:41:50,570 --> 00:41:53,610
This is often referred to
as goal-conditioned RL.

822
00:41:53,610 --> 00:41:55,130
But then once you
do this, you're

823
00:41:55,130 --> 00:42:00,600
basically getting a modified MDP
that covers each of these tasks.

824
00:42:00,600 --> 00:42:03,650

825
00:42:03,650 --> 00:42:05,880
Now, in terms of the
reward, like we talked about

826
00:42:05,880 --> 00:42:10,760
on the board, it's basically
going to be the same as before.

827
00:42:10,760 --> 00:42:13,160
In this special case of
goal-conditioned reinforcement

828
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:17,560
learning, where you're trying to
reach a goal, then oftentimes,

829
00:42:17,560 --> 00:42:22,280
you'll think about the reward
as some negative distance

830
00:42:22,280 --> 00:42:27,010
between the state that you're
currently at and the goal state.

831
00:42:27,010 --> 00:42:31,233

832
00:42:31,233 --> 00:42:32,900
And distance functions
can be Euclidean.

833
00:42:32,900 --> 00:42:36,060
It could be a sparse
0, 1 and so forth.

834
00:42:36,060 --> 00:42:39,960
So we still get kind of a
standard Markov decision process

835
00:42:39,960 --> 00:42:43,800
when we bring these
tasks together.

836
00:42:43,800 --> 00:42:47,520
And you might ask, if we can get
this standard Markov decision

837
00:42:47,520 --> 00:42:50,440
process that now has a task
identifier, like a task string,

838
00:42:50,440 --> 00:42:53,680
as part of the state
space, then why not

839
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:58,440
apply standard reinforcement
learning algorithms to them?

840
00:42:58,440 --> 00:43:01,080
And the answer is, you can.

841
00:43:01,080 --> 00:43:04,320
And in many cases,
this is a great way

842
00:43:04,320 --> 00:43:10,208
to approach scenarios where you
want to learn multiple tasks.

843
00:43:10,208 --> 00:43:12,000
But the reason why I'm
bringing all this up

844
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:13,760
is that in some cases,
you can actually

845
00:43:13,760 --> 00:43:18,400
do better by leveraging
the known structure

846
00:43:18,400 --> 00:43:20,560
between different tasks.

847
00:43:20,560 --> 00:43:22,480
And goal-conditioned RL
is one of those cases

848
00:43:22,480 --> 00:43:24,840
where you're trying to
reach different goals.

849
00:43:24,840 --> 00:43:27,280
And in the next
few slides, we'll

850
00:43:27,280 --> 00:43:29,960
talk about one
particular way that you

851
00:43:29,960 --> 00:43:33,640
can do a lot better by
leveraging this fact

852
00:43:33,640 --> 00:43:37,520
that this MDP consists
of these different tasks

853
00:43:37,520 --> 00:43:39,100
that you're trying to do.

854
00:43:39,100 --> 00:43:39,600
Yeah.

855
00:43:39,600 --> 00:43:41,500
Euclidean L2, that's
just mean squared error.

856
00:43:41,500 --> 00:43:42,400
Yeah.

857
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:45,740
So the question is, if different
people are different tasks,

858
00:43:45,740 --> 00:43:49,400
then is there a better way
to represent different people

859
00:43:49,400 --> 00:43:51,440
than a one hot--

860
00:43:51,440 --> 00:43:56,938
when I say one hot, by the
way, I mean a vector like this

861
00:43:56,938 --> 00:43:58,980
where, if you're going to
represent the number 2,

862
00:43:58,980 --> 00:44:02,980
you use a one-hot vector.

863
00:44:02,980 --> 00:44:06,670
So yeah, there are definitely
better ways than just this.

864
00:44:06,670 --> 00:44:10,950
This is actually fairly common
in a lot of reinforcement

865
00:44:10,950 --> 00:44:13,190
learning methods.

866
00:44:13,190 --> 00:44:15,892
For example, one
thing you could do

867
00:44:15,892 --> 00:44:17,350
is, you could try
to get a language

868
00:44:17,350 --> 00:44:20,990
description of the person
or their preferences.

869
00:44:20,990 --> 00:44:24,310
You could also--
this gets into stuff

870
00:44:24,310 --> 00:44:26,250
that we'll talk about next week.

871
00:44:26,250 --> 00:44:29,190
But you could also use
some examples of things

872
00:44:29,190 --> 00:44:31,910
that they like and don't like
as part of the task descriptor

873
00:44:31,910 --> 00:44:32,750
as well.

874
00:44:32,750 --> 00:44:36,310
And so you get something
more like in-context learning

875
00:44:36,310 --> 00:44:40,270
in that case, where zi actually
might contain some data

876
00:44:40,270 --> 00:44:42,070
about the task.

877
00:44:42,070 --> 00:44:46,510
So yeah, there are some
better examples like that.

878
00:44:46,510 --> 00:44:48,970
So in terms of
this problem setup,

879
00:44:48,970 --> 00:44:50,790
let's actually
look at an example.

880
00:44:50,790 --> 00:44:53,230
So we can just start with
multitask imitation learning.

881
00:44:53,230 --> 00:44:56,510
And in the multitask
imitation learning

882
00:44:56,510 --> 00:45:03,500
setting, we can think of it as,
if we have different tasks, i,

883
00:45:03,500 --> 00:45:08,100
if the number of tasks, i, is
a relatively small number, then

884
00:45:08,100 --> 00:45:11,020
you can basically
think of it as having

885
00:45:11,020 --> 00:45:15,540
some set of demonstrations that
are demonstration trajectories

886
00:45:15,540 --> 00:45:18,700
for each task,
where each of these

887
00:45:18,700 --> 00:45:21,080
contains expert demonstrations.

888
00:45:21,080 --> 00:45:24,340

889
00:45:24,340 --> 00:45:30,340
So these are demos for task i.

890
00:45:30,340 --> 00:45:39,410
And this is for i equals 1 to N
if you have N different tasks.

891
00:45:39,410 --> 00:45:42,460

892
00:45:42,460 --> 00:45:45,300
And if we were to
think about how

893
00:45:45,300 --> 00:45:49,700
to use this data for
imitation learning,

894
00:45:49,700 --> 00:45:53,425
thinking back to
the second lecture,

895
00:45:53,425 --> 00:45:54,800
we were doing
imitation learning.

896
00:45:54,800 --> 00:45:58,940
We had this objective of
minimizing the log probability

897
00:45:58,940 --> 00:46:01,170
of actions' given states.

898
00:46:01,170 --> 00:46:07,130
And if we just write this as
minimizing the supervised loss

899
00:46:07,130 --> 00:46:11,650
function with respect to
our policy parameters,

900
00:46:11,650 --> 00:46:15,490
then when we want to move to
the multitask setting, what

901
00:46:15,490 --> 00:46:20,610
we can do is, we can simply try
to now minimize a single policy

902
00:46:20,610 --> 00:46:24,890
set of policy parameters over
the loss function averaged

903
00:46:24,890 --> 00:46:26,860
over all of the tasks.

904
00:46:26,860 --> 00:46:31,810

905
00:46:31,810 --> 00:46:35,270
So this is basically like
multitask imitation learning,

906
00:46:35,270 --> 00:46:39,170
pretty simple, hopefully.

907
00:46:39,170 --> 00:46:42,690
One useful trick for
this sort of approach

908
00:46:42,690 --> 00:46:48,587
is that if tasks are very
different, like if one task is

909
00:46:48,587 --> 00:46:50,170
telling you to go
forward, one task is

910
00:46:50,170 --> 00:46:52,010
telling you to go backwards--

911
00:46:52,010 --> 00:46:53,770
maybe one task is
telling you to lie down.

912
00:46:53,770 --> 00:46:56,530
They're telling the policy
to do very different things.

913
00:46:56,530 --> 00:46:59,890
Then if you sampled one
mini batch that's mostly

914
00:46:59,890 --> 00:47:03,490
going forward and a different
mini batch of mostly laying

915
00:47:03,490 --> 00:47:07,470
down, for example, then from
one gradient step to the next,

916
00:47:07,470 --> 00:47:09,470
you're actually going to
have a lot of variance.

917
00:47:09,470 --> 00:47:10,570
Because the first
mini batch is going

918
00:47:10,570 --> 00:47:11,987
to tell you to do
something that's

919
00:47:11,987 --> 00:47:14,192
pretty different from
the second mini batch.

920
00:47:14,192 --> 00:47:15,650
And hopefully, the
policy should be

921
00:47:15,650 --> 00:47:19,210
able to use the task identifier
to differentiate the two.

922
00:47:19,210 --> 00:47:21,490
But in some cases, it
can be a little bit

923
00:47:21,490 --> 00:47:23,970
tricky for the
network to do that.

924
00:47:23,970 --> 00:47:25,610
And so one thing
that's quite useful

925
00:47:25,610 --> 00:47:27,570
is that when you
sample a mini batch,

926
00:47:27,570 --> 00:47:30,170
you can stratify the
mini batch and ensure

927
00:47:30,170 --> 00:47:35,410
that each mini batch of data
has data from each task.

928
00:47:35,410 --> 00:47:38,450
And if you're trying to optimize
for uniform distribution

929
00:47:38,450 --> 00:47:41,450
over your tasks, then your
mini batch would be like--

930
00:47:41,450 --> 00:47:43,870
1/n of the data would
be the first task.

931
00:47:43,870 --> 00:47:47,410
1/n of the mini batch would be
the second task and so forth.

932
00:47:47,410 --> 00:47:51,090
And this will give you gradients
that are lower variance.

933
00:47:51,090 --> 00:47:53,810
And it can be quite
useful in practice

934
00:47:53,810 --> 00:47:55,662
to do something like this.

935
00:47:55,662 --> 00:47:58,120
Another thing that I didn't
put on the slides that can also

936
00:47:58,120 --> 00:48:02,120
be useful is making sure that
your states and actions are

937
00:48:02,120 --> 00:48:04,220
on similar scales across tasks.

938
00:48:04,220 --> 00:48:07,240
So per-task
normalization can also

939
00:48:07,240 --> 00:48:10,210
be quite useful for
the learning process.

940
00:48:10,210 --> 00:48:13,920

941
00:48:13,920 --> 00:48:19,120
Now, we've talked about
training this multitask policy.

942
00:48:19,120 --> 00:48:22,560
And when we do that,
we're now going

943
00:48:22,560 --> 00:48:28,200
to have a policy that takes as
input some state and some task

944
00:48:28,200 --> 00:48:34,480
descriptor and then needs
to output some action.

945
00:48:34,480 --> 00:48:36,400
And you might have
scenarios where

946
00:48:36,400 --> 00:48:40,820
this is like a language
string or a one-hot vector

947
00:48:40,820 --> 00:48:41,920
and this is an image.

948
00:48:41,920 --> 00:48:45,220
And that's inherently a
very multimodal problem.

949
00:48:45,220 --> 00:48:46,940
You have different
data modalities.

950
00:48:46,940 --> 00:48:49,010
These are very
different dimensions.

951
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:50,760
And so in practice,
there's a question of,

952
00:48:50,760 --> 00:48:52,760
how do you design
architectures that

953
00:48:52,760 --> 00:48:55,750
take as input this kind
of multimodal data?

954
00:48:55,750 --> 00:48:58,390

955
00:48:58,390 --> 00:49:02,150
One example that I'll give
that I like because it actually

956
00:49:02,150 --> 00:49:06,030
uses multiple different
modalities of task descriptors

957
00:49:06,030 --> 00:49:09,830
is this paper here, where
the goal is a multitask

958
00:49:09,830 --> 00:49:12,310
imitation learning system,
is training a robot

959
00:49:12,310 --> 00:49:15,430
to do some simple
manipulation tasks.

960
00:49:15,430 --> 00:49:18,190
And the way the
architecture works--

961
00:49:18,190 --> 00:49:21,430
this paper is a little bit old,
so it's not using a transformer.

962
00:49:21,430 --> 00:49:23,670
But here's the current
state right here.

963
00:49:23,670 --> 00:49:25,910
It actually
downsamples the image

964
00:49:25,910 --> 00:49:28,430
before passing it
into the model.

965
00:49:28,430 --> 00:49:31,730
And then it also uses, actually,
two different task descriptors.

966
00:49:31,730 --> 00:49:35,390
So it uses a language string as
one task descriptor, which is

967
00:49:35,390 --> 00:49:37,990
put bottle in the ceramic bowl.

968
00:49:37,990 --> 00:49:39,990
And it also trains
the model to be

969
00:49:39,990 --> 00:49:42,050
able to, instead of
using the language,

970
00:49:42,050 --> 00:49:44,893
be able to use a video
of how to do the task.

971
00:49:44,893 --> 00:49:46,310
In this case, the
video is a video

972
00:49:46,310 --> 00:49:51,310
of a person placing
the bottle in the bowl.

973
00:49:51,310 --> 00:49:56,140
And so you can design models
that in this case then,

974
00:49:56,140 --> 00:50:01,260
for the language, use a language
encoder, like things that

975
00:50:01,260 --> 00:50:04,960
are typically used in
language models, for example,

976
00:50:04,960 --> 00:50:05,502
transformers.

977
00:50:05,502 --> 00:50:07,460
And actually, I think
the language encoder here

978
00:50:07,460 --> 00:50:08,980
did actually use a transformer.

979
00:50:08,980 --> 00:50:10,760
And then, separately,
for the video,

980
00:50:10,760 --> 00:50:12,780
it would be using a
different kind of encoder

981
00:50:12,780 --> 00:50:14,960
because video data
looks very different.

982
00:50:14,960 --> 00:50:18,000
And it uses a video encoder
for the second part.

983
00:50:18,000 --> 00:50:23,580
And then it kind of
multiplies these embeddings

984
00:50:23,580 --> 00:50:26,680
into different layers
of the network,

985
00:50:26,680 --> 00:50:29,790
the backbone of the network
that processes the state.

986
00:50:29,790 --> 00:50:33,700

987
00:50:33,700 --> 00:50:36,460
And then the loss
function corresponds

988
00:50:36,460 --> 00:50:39,020
to a behavior-cloning loss.

989
00:50:39,020 --> 00:50:42,540
And it either samples
the task descriptor

990
00:50:42,540 --> 00:50:45,420
from the video encoder or
from the language encoder.

991
00:50:45,420 --> 00:50:50,060
And then the whole model,
including these encoders,

992
00:50:50,060 --> 00:50:53,120
is trained using the
behavior-cloning loss.

993
00:50:53,120 --> 00:50:56,620

994
00:50:56,620 --> 00:50:59,900
So this is one
practical example.

995
00:50:59,900 --> 00:51:02,600
Now, we can also look at some
more modern architectures.

996
00:51:02,600 --> 00:51:05,700
And first off, if
you have something

997
00:51:05,700 --> 00:51:08,800
like a language model,
the task descriptor,

998
00:51:08,800 --> 00:51:11,380
it's very natural for it
just to be a language string.

999
00:51:11,380 --> 00:51:13,800
And you can think of that
basically just as the prompt.

1000
00:51:13,800 --> 00:51:18,580
And so one way to think about
multitask imitation learning is,

1001
00:51:18,580 --> 00:51:21,140
basically, all of the language
model imitation learning

1002
00:51:21,140 --> 00:51:23,520
or supervised fine-tuning
is with different prompts.

1003
00:51:23,520 --> 00:51:26,220
You can sort of think of
those as different tasks.

1004
00:51:26,220 --> 00:51:30,380
And it's very easy to just
pass that into a transformer.

1005
00:51:30,380 --> 00:51:33,380
And in terms of modern
policy architectures

1006
00:51:33,380 --> 00:51:36,420
for using task
strings in robotics,

1007
00:51:36,420 --> 00:51:39,540
it's often common to
also use a transformer

1008
00:51:39,540 --> 00:51:43,520
and find ways to
use image encoders,

1009
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:47,620
in this case, pre-trained
image encoders, project

1010
00:51:47,620 --> 00:51:51,140
those into the input
space of a language model,

1011
00:51:51,140 --> 00:51:53,810
also use the task
descriptor here,

1012
00:51:53,810 --> 00:51:55,690
which is, what should
the robot do to put

1013
00:51:55,690 --> 00:51:57,130
the eggplant in the bowl?

1014
00:51:57,130 --> 00:52:00,170
Pass those in as tokens
to a language model.

1015
00:52:00,170 --> 00:52:03,730
And then output the actions
and train the whole model,

1016
00:52:03,730 --> 00:52:09,330
including the image encoder and
the text encoder end to end.

1017
00:52:09,330 --> 00:52:14,050
So here, you can view
the task identifier zi

1018
00:52:14,050 --> 00:52:18,210
as basically the
prompt for the model.

1019
00:52:18,210 --> 00:52:19,810
The question is, how
does this compare

1020
00:52:19,810 --> 00:52:26,113
to traditional
reinforcement learning

1021
00:52:26,113 --> 00:52:28,030
in terms of task performance
and success rate?

1022
00:52:28,030 --> 00:52:31,850

1023
00:52:31,850 --> 00:52:35,150
So I guess there's a couple of
things that are different here.

1024
00:52:35,150 --> 00:52:41,130
We'll talk about multitask RL
in a bit, right after this.

1025
00:52:41,130 --> 00:52:47,043
And then so you can do this sort
of prompt conditioning, task

1026
00:52:47,043 --> 00:52:49,210
identifier conditioning
both with imitation learning

1027
00:52:49,210 --> 00:52:51,760
and with reinforcement learning.

1028
00:52:51,760 --> 00:52:54,240
And generally,
the considerations

1029
00:52:54,240 --> 00:52:55,760
about imitation
learning versus RL

1030
00:52:55,760 --> 00:52:58,200
is very similar to what
we've discussed so far, where

1031
00:52:58,200 --> 00:53:00,320
with imitation
learning, you're not

1032
00:53:00,320 --> 00:53:03,340
able to use a reward function
to improve over the expert data.

1033
00:53:03,340 --> 00:53:05,200
But it's very simple
and it's very easy

1034
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:08,160
to scale simple things
to large data sets,

1035
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:11,980
whereas RL has a lot of
potential and can help a lot

1036
00:53:11,980 --> 00:53:14,240
when you have some
reward information

1037
00:53:14,240 --> 00:53:16,420
and you want to
optimize those rewards.

1038
00:53:16,420 --> 00:53:17,920
And you can actually
do a lot better

1039
00:53:17,920 --> 00:53:22,800
than some provided data set.

1040
00:53:22,800 --> 00:53:25,360
And then just some
examples of what

1041
00:53:25,360 --> 00:53:29,040
multitask imitation learning
policies might look like--

1042
00:53:29,040 --> 00:53:33,073
the examples from those
two prior architectures--

1043
00:53:33,073 --> 00:53:34,740
the robots are doing
very simple things.

1044
00:53:34,740 --> 00:53:35,760
And so I wanted
to pick something

1045
00:53:35,760 --> 00:53:37,120
that's a bit more recent.

1046
00:53:37,120 --> 00:53:41,080
So here, the task identifier
is in the top left.

1047
00:53:41,080 --> 00:53:42,860
In this video, there'll
just be two tasks.

1048
00:53:42,860 --> 00:53:45,720
One is to flatten the towel,
and one is to hang the towel.

1049
00:53:45,720 --> 00:53:49,280
And so for the first part, the
task is to flatten the towel.

1050
00:53:49,280 --> 00:53:51,280
And then here, we're
actually stringing together

1051
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:52,800
multiple tasks.

1052
00:53:52,800 --> 00:53:56,855
So at the point where the robot
is at a point where the towel is

1053
00:53:56,855 --> 00:53:59,480
in a more flattened state, the
human actually switches the task

1054
00:53:59,480 --> 00:54:03,480
and then tells the robot, now
I want you to hang the towel.

1055
00:54:03,480 --> 00:54:07,760
And this, again, is passed into
a transformer for the robot

1056
00:54:07,760 --> 00:54:09,960
to do that task.

1057
00:54:09,960 --> 00:54:12,045
So this is a case where
there's two tasks.

1058
00:54:12,045 --> 00:54:14,420
You can also do, actually, a
lot more tasks, potentially.

1059
00:54:14,420 --> 00:54:17,400
So here's an example where
there's I think more than 10

1060
00:54:17,400 --> 00:54:19,320
tasks, 10 different
language strings

1061
00:54:19,320 --> 00:54:23,520
that the robot follows, so like,
put the plate in the drawer.

1062
00:54:23,520 --> 00:54:24,500
Close the microwave.

1063
00:54:24,500 --> 00:54:25,500
Drive the base backward.

1064
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:26,760
Rotate the base left.

1065
00:54:26,760 --> 00:54:28,968
And one of the things that's
interesting to note here

1066
00:54:28,968 --> 00:54:31,560
is that tasks can also have
different levels of abstraction.

1067
00:54:31,560 --> 00:54:33,227
So you might have a
level of abstraction

1068
00:54:33,227 --> 00:54:36,503
of just like, drive forward or
move your right gripper lower.

1069
00:54:36,503 --> 00:54:37,920
Or you might have
something that's

1070
00:54:37,920 --> 00:54:41,160
much higher, like
throw the napkin away

1071
00:54:41,160 --> 00:54:44,000
or something like that.

1072
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:47,240
The question is, if you had
one policy for each task,

1073
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:50,050
would that run similar
to this or different?

1074
00:54:50,050 --> 00:54:50,550
Yeah.

1075
00:54:50,550 --> 00:54:54,050
So in this case, it's one
policy with the prompt as input.

1076
00:54:54,050 --> 00:54:57,830
You could also have a different
policy, one for each task.

1077
00:54:57,830 --> 00:54:59,830
If you don't do any--

1078
00:54:59,830 --> 00:55:02,372
what I would call weight
sharing, like if you have,

1079
00:55:02,372 --> 00:55:03,830
basically, completely
different set

1080
00:55:03,830 --> 00:55:05,990
of parameters for
every single task,

1081
00:55:05,990 --> 00:55:09,230
then you can't
really make use of--

1082
00:55:09,230 --> 00:55:11,610
the weights are only trained
on data from that one task.

1083
00:55:11,610 --> 00:55:13,443
And you're going to
have a smaller data set.

1084
00:55:13,443 --> 00:55:15,310
And so usually,
in many scenarios

1085
00:55:15,310 --> 00:55:18,590
that performs worse than
training a single policy.

1086
00:55:18,590 --> 00:55:22,030
Another consideration is that it
ends up using a lot more memory

1087
00:55:22,030 --> 00:55:23,450
to store all those weights.

1088
00:55:23,450 --> 00:55:26,570
And you might actually need to
reload the model into memory.

1089
00:55:26,570 --> 00:55:30,223
And so it can take longer to
switch between different tasks.

1090
00:55:30,223 --> 00:55:32,390
But sometimes there are
also optimization challenges

1091
00:55:32,390 --> 00:55:34,950
with trying to fit multiple
tasks into one model.

1092
00:55:34,950 --> 00:55:37,030
And so sometimes there
are some circumstances

1093
00:55:37,030 --> 00:55:39,350
where training different
policies for different tasks

1094
00:55:39,350 --> 00:55:40,610
can be useful.

1095
00:55:40,610 --> 00:55:41,110
Yeah.

1096
00:55:41,110 --> 00:55:43,570
So the question is, are
these systems hierarchical?

1097
00:55:43,570 --> 00:55:47,070
Like, some of these tasks
are fairly high level.

1098
00:55:47,070 --> 00:55:49,620
So the next part
of this slide is,

1099
00:55:49,620 --> 00:55:51,347
what about even more
high-level tasks?

1100
00:55:51,347 --> 00:55:53,930
Like, what if you wanted to tell
the robot, clean the kitchen?

1101
00:55:53,930 --> 00:55:57,660

1102
00:55:57,660 --> 00:55:59,680
For the videos that
I've shown here,

1103
00:55:59,680 --> 00:56:02,940
this is just one single
policy that's doing it.

1104
00:56:02,940 --> 00:56:08,040
But hierarchy is very useful
for higher-level tasks.

1105
00:56:08,040 --> 00:56:10,340
And if you just told this
robot to clean the kitchen,

1106
00:56:10,340 --> 00:56:14,100
it wouldn't be able to do
what it did in this video.

1107
00:56:14,100 --> 00:56:16,780
And so we'll have a lecture
on hierarchy in, I think,

1108
00:56:16,780 --> 00:56:18,540
two-ish weeks.

1109
00:56:18,540 --> 00:56:20,900
And we'll talk about
exactly that and scenarios

1110
00:56:20,900 --> 00:56:24,900
where we could try to do
even higher-level tasks.

1111
00:56:24,900 --> 00:56:27,420
But yeah, even with a single
policy, a single flat policy

1112
00:56:27,420 --> 00:56:32,580
without hierarchy, it is able to
do reasonable actions for tasks

1113
00:56:32,580 --> 00:56:34,780
that are somewhat high level.

1114
00:56:34,780 --> 00:56:36,500
What does long-horizon
task mean?

1115
00:56:36,500 --> 00:56:40,500
By that, I mean a task that
takes a long time, basically,

1116
00:56:40,500 --> 00:56:42,228
where you takes many time steps.

1117
00:56:42,228 --> 00:56:44,020
You have to take a lot
of different actions

1118
00:56:44,020 --> 00:56:45,660
in order to do that.

1119
00:56:45,660 --> 00:56:47,770
And there's generally
a number of challenges

1120
00:56:47,770 --> 00:56:50,210
that can come up with
long-horizon tasks, such

1121
00:56:50,210 --> 00:56:56,370
as you have to be thinking
about what you should be doing

1122
00:56:56,370 --> 00:56:57,735
in order to complete that.

1123
00:56:57,735 --> 00:57:00,110
And there's lots of things
you could do to complete that.

1124
00:57:00,110 --> 00:57:01,652
So there might be multimodality.

1125
00:57:01,652 --> 00:57:03,110
In reinforcement
learning settings,

1126
00:57:03,110 --> 00:57:05,150
you might also have
sparse rewards.

1127
00:57:05,150 --> 00:57:07,910
And you might not get reward
feedback for a very long time.

1128
00:57:07,910 --> 00:57:08,410
Yeah.

1129
00:57:08,410 --> 00:57:10,790
So the question is, a lot of
these tasks are pretty similar.

1130
00:57:10,790 --> 00:57:12,630
What if you wanted a task
that's more vision based--

1131
00:57:12,630 --> 00:57:13,338
I think you said.

1132
00:57:13,338 --> 00:57:15,850

1133
00:57:15,850 --> 00:57:16,350
Yeah.

1134
00:57:16,350 --> 00:57:18,730
So this particular
model was actually

1135
00:57:18,730 --> 00:57:20,770
also trained to do
certain vision tasks,

1136
00:57:20,770 --> 00:57:23,450
like asking questions
about the image

1137
00:57:23,450 --> 00:57:27,650
and captioning an image,
that sort of stuff.

1138
00:57:27,650 --> 00:57:31,070
And if you have a
big enough model,

1139
00:57:31,070 --> 00:57:32,330
it's not too hard to do that.

1140
00:57:32,330 --> 00:57:34,170
But generally, you
do need larger models

1141
00:57:34,170 --> 00:57:35,720
to be able to do more things.

1142
00:57:35,720 --> 00:57:38,490

1143
00:57:38,490 --> 00:57:39,373
Cool.

1144
00:57:39,373 --> 00:57:41,790
So we've talked a bit about
multi-task imitation learning.

1145
00:57:41,790 --> 00:57:44,730
Now what about multitask RL?

1146
00:57:44,730 --> 00:57:47,890
Basically, the story
is pretty similar.

1147
00:57:47,890 --> 00:57:51,330
We can feed as input a task
descriptor into the policy,

1148
00:57:51,330 --> 00:57:54,650
feed as input a task descriptor
into a Q function or a value

1149
00:57:54,650 --> 00:57:55,150
function.

1150
00:57:55,150 --> 00:57:56,810
Whatever we're
learning, we can pass

1151
00:57:56,810 --> 00:58:01,610
in that prompt or some
other descriptor of the task

1152
00:58:01,610 --> 00:58:02,460
into the models.

1153
00:58:02,460 --> 00:58:05,330

1154
00:58:05,330 --> 00:58:08,890
If you have a discrete
number of tasks,

1155
00:58:08,890 --> 00:58:10,890
then it can be useful to
have a different replay

1156
00:58:10,890 --> 00:58:12,850
buffer for each task,
especially if you

1157
00:58:12,850 --> 00:58:15,360
want to try to balance the
sampling of each batch.

1158
00:58:15,360 --> 00:58:19,090

1159
00:58:19,090 --> 00:58:23,410
And now there's this
question of sharing data.

1160
00:58:23,410 --> 00:58:26,610
And this comes up specifically
in reinforcement learning,

1161
00:58:26,610 --> 00:58:27,830
not in imitation learning.

1162
00:58:27,830 --> 00:58:30,330
So in particular,
if we collected

1163
00:58:30,330 --> 00:58:35,570
some sort of exploratory
data for a task when-- like,

1164
00:58:35,570 --> 00:58:38,610
maybe our policy was told
to pick up the sponge

1165
00:58:38,610 --> 00:58:40,170
or something like that.

1166
00:58:40,170 --> 00:58:41,507
Our policy did something.

1167
00:58:41,507 --> 00:58:43,090
It might not have
picked up the sponge

1168
00:58:43,090 --> 00:58:45,440
because it might
have made a mistake.

1169
00:58:45,440 --> 00:58:49,880
That sort of experience might
be useful for other task

1170
00:58:49,880 --> 00:58:51,240
descriptors as well.

1171
00:58:51,240 --> 00:58:55,342
And if we have a per-task replay
buffer, like one for each task,

1172
00:58:55,342 --> 00:58:56,800
then we're not
actually going to be

1173
00:58:56,800 --> 00:58:59,808
updating the policy for
other tasks using that data.

1174
00:58:59,808 --> 00:59:01,600
And we could actually
think about scenarios

1175
00:59:01,600 --> 00:59:05,720
where we might actually be able
to reuse data from one task

1176
00:59:05,720 --> 00:59:12,612
to actually more explicitly
help the model or the policy

1177
00:59:12,612 --> 00:59:14,070
learn something
about another task.

1178
00:59:14,070 --> 00:59:16,577

1179
00:59:16,577 --> 00:59:18,160
And if we were to
do this, we actually

1180
00:59:18,160 --> 00:59:20,080
have to relabel the data.

1181
00:59:20,080 --> 00:59:25,600
So specifically, if the tasks
have different reward functions

1182
00:59:25,600 --> 00:59:28,000
but maybe the same
dynamics, then you'd

1183
00:59:28,000 --> 00:59:29,480
have to actually
change the reward

1184
00:59:29,480 --> 00:59:32,600
label in the data set
for it to be the reward

1185
00:59:32,600 --> 00:59:35,000
label for the other task.

1186
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:36,660
So let's walk
through an example.

1187
00:59:36,660 --> 00:59:41,910
So say that you are trying
to learn to play hockey.

1188
00:59:41,910 --> 00:59:48,030
And there's maybe some
teammates in blue.

1189
00:59:48,030 --> 00:59:52,110
There is a goalkeeper from
the other team in orange.

1190
00:59:52,110 --> 00:59:54,930
And maybe one task is passing.

1191
00:59:54,930 --> 00:59:57,990
Another task is shooting a goal.

1192
00:59:57,990 --> 01:00:02,150
And maybe you are trying to
shoot a goal, but you messed up

1193
01:00:02,150 --> 01:00:07,030
and you accidentally actually
making a pretty good pass

1194
01:00:07,030 --> 01:00:10,710
to your teammate.

1195
01:00:10,710 --> 01:00:13,767
Then that data that you just
collected, even though you

1196
01:00:13,767 --> 01:00:16,350
were intending to shoot a goal,
that data actually is probably

1197
01:00:16,350 --> 01:00:21,047
pretty useful for learning
how to be better at passing.

1198
01:00:21,047 --> 01:00:22,630
And so what you could
do is, you could

1199
01:00:22,630 --> 01:00:25,470
store that experience as
normal under the original task

1200
01:00:25,470 --> 01:00:26,390
identifier.

1201
01:00:26,390 --> 01:00:30,990
But you could also relabel
the experience with task

1202
01:00:30,990 --> 01:00:35,105
with the task 1
ID, specifically,

1203
01:00:35,105 --> 01:00:36,730
the passing ID--
there's a typo there--

1204
01:00:36,730 --> 01:00:41,100
and then store that in the
replay buffer for that task.

1205
01:00:41,100 --> 01:00:48,550
And so specifically what this
algorithm would look like is--

1206
01:00:48,550 --> 01:00:51,060

1207
01:00:51,060 --> 01:00:56,620
maybe you pick
one of your tasks.

1208
01:00:56,620 --> 01:01:00,620
And then we pick a
corresponding task identifier.

1209
01:01:00,620 --> 01:01:04,750
Then we're going
to collect data.

1210
01:01:04,750 --> 01:01:07,340

1211
01:01:07,340 --> 01:01:12,820
And we'll be collecting
data from our policy.

1212
01:01:12,820 --> 01:01:14,460
That's going to be
then conditioned

1213
01:01:14,460 --> 01:01:18,060
on that task identifier.

1214
01:01:18,060 --> 01:01:20,620

1215
01:01:20,620 --> 01:01:24,020
And maybe let's refer
to this data as--

1216
01:01:24,020 --> 01:01:31,700
it'll have st,
at, st plus 1, rt.

1217
01:01:31,700 --> 01:01:35,920
And then it will also
have the task identifier.

1218
01:01:35,920 --> 01:01:41,680
We'll maybe put this into
a replay buffer for task i.

1219
01:01:41,680 --> 01:01:45,170
So we'll store this
in the replay buffer.

1220
01:01:45,170 --> 01:01:48,020

1221
01:01:48,020 --> 01:01:50,540
And then we could actually
then potentially use

1222
01:01:50,540 --> 01:01:53,620
this for another task as well.

1223
01:01:53,620 --> 01:01:58,740
So that's what I'm
calling relabeling.

1224
01:01:58,740 --> 01:02:04,798
And we could consider this
data-- so st, at, st plus 1.

1225
01:02:04,798 --> 01:02:07,340
But we could actually consider
it for a different task, maybe

1226
01:02:07,340 --> 01:02:08,300
task j.

1227
01:02:08,300 --> 01:02:11,100
And so the reward
for task j would

1228
01:02:11,100 --> 01:02:19,900
be r of j for st, at and the
task identifier for task j.

1229
01:02:19,900 --> 01:02:22,620
And we could take this
data that's been relabeled

1230
01:02:22,620 --> 01:02:26,300
and add this to either
our shared replay

1231
01:02:26,300 --> 01:02:30,540
buffer or our replay
buffer for task j.

1232
01:02:30,540 --> 01:02:34,500
And so then we're actually
using experience for one task

1233
01:02:34,500 --> 01:02:39,130
to learn another task.

1234
01:02:39,130 --> 01:02:44,970
And then we might do
this for multiple tasks.

1235
01:02:44,970 --> 01:02:50,330
Eventually, we will update
our policy, pi theta,

1236
01:02:50,330 --> 01:02:54,110
and collect some more data.

1237
01:02:54,110 --> 01:02:54,610
Yeah.

1238
01:02:54,610 --> 01:02:57,230
So the question is, how do we
decide if it's worth relabeling?

1239
01:02:57,230 --> 01:03:00,770
And in particular, if we're
doing this for task j,

1240
01:03:00,770 --> 01:03:03,203
which task j should we pick?

1241
01:03:03,203 --> 01:03:04,370
Should we do it for all j's?

1242
01:03:04,370 --> 01:03:06,710
Should we only do
it for some j's?

1243
01:03:06,710 --> 01:03:09,250
Should we only do
this some of the time?

1244
01:03:09,250 --> 01:03:12,230
This ends up being somewhat
kind of a heuristic process.

1245
01:03:12,230 --> 01:03:15,570
Because it's a little bit
hard to tell, generally,

1246
01:03:15,570 --> 01:03:18,210
if data will be useful
for learning one task

1247
01:03:18,210 --> 01:03:20,290
or not because
learning is somewhat

1248
01:03:20,290 --> 01:03:23,170
of an amorphous process.

1249
01:03:23,170 --> 01:03:28,330
The one heuristic is
that if our j was high,

1250
01:03:28,330 --> 01:03:30,690
then it might be good
for learning that task.

1251
01:03:30,690 --> 01:03:34,210
Because you actually might have
accidentally done something good

1252
01:03:34,210 --> 01:03:35,690
for a different task.

1253
01:03:35,690 --> 01:03:37,760
So that's one heuristic.

1254
01:03:37,760 --> 01:03:41,360
Another heuristic sometimes is
just to also sample randomly

1255
01:03:41,360 --> 01:03:45,240
and use it for all tasks
at random and uniformly.

1256
01:03:45,240 --> 01:03:47,880
How does this work
for continuous state?

1257
01:03:47,880 --> 01:03:51,520
There's nothing here that
assumes discrete states.

1258
01:03:51,520 --> 01:03:52,660
How do you reach--

1259
01:03:52,660 --> 01:03:57,400
so what I've written here is
like a relabeling algorithm

1260
01:03:57,400 --> 01:04:01,280
for any sort of
multitask system.

1261
01:04:01,280 --> 01:04:04,280
We'll talk about what this looks
like for goal-condition settings

1262
01:04:04,280 --> 01:04:07,540
in a minute if that's what
you're wondering about.

1263
01:04:07,540 --> 01:04:08,040
Yeah.

1264
01:04:08,040 --> 01:04:09,540
So this is a great point.

1265
01:04:09,540 --> 01:04:12,240

1266
01:04:12,240 --> 01:04:14,520
You can't do this universally.

1267
01:04:14,520 --> 01:04:18,520
And so this is something
that's called hindsight

1268
01:04:18,520 --> 01:04:20,765
relabeling because you
look at what you did.

1269
01:04:20,765 --> 01:04:22,640
If you did something
good, then in hindsight,

1270
01:04:22,640 --> 01:04:26,440
you can then relabel that
to be a different task.

1271
01:04:26,440 --> 01:04:30,080
So in terms of this
algorithm on the slides,

1272
01:04:30,080 --> 01:04:32,140
we collect some data
using some policy.

1273
01:04:32,140 --> 01:04:34,760
We store that data
in a replay buffer.

1274
01:04:34,760 --> 01:04:37,630
We are going to perform
hindsight relabeling.

1275
01:04:37,630 --> 01:04:40,350
And what this looks
like is, we originally

1276
01:04:40,350 --> 01:04:42,190
collected it for task i.

1277
01:04:42,190 --> 01:04:47,483
We're going to relabel it
with the reward for task j.

1278
01:04:47,483 --> 01:04:49,150
And then we'll store
that relabeled data

1279
01:04:49,150 --> 01:04:50,510
in a replay buffer.

1280
01:04:50,510 --> 01:04:54,863
The slides have this for a
single combined replay buffer.

1281
01:04:54,863 --> 01:04:57,030
You could also do this with
different replay buffers

1282
01:04:57,030 --> 01:04:58,870
for each task.

1283
01:04:58,870 --> 01:05:01,270
Then you'll update your
policy with the replay buffer

1284
01:05:01,270 --> 01:05:04,670
and repeat this process.

1285
01:05:04,670 --> 01:05:06,310
In terms of which
tasks to choose,

1286
01:05:06,310 --> 01:05:10,405
you can choose this randomly,
task to get high rewards.

1287
01:05:10,405 --> 01:05:12,030
The note here is
also, you can actually

1288
01:05:12,030 --> 01:05:14,090
do it for trajectories
that get high rewards,

1289
01:05:14,090 --> 01:05:16,350
not just transitions
that get high rewards.

1290
01:05:16,350 --> 01:05:19,130
Because if there's a high reward
somewhere in the trajectory,

1291
01:05:19,130 --> 01:05:20,547
then other parts
of the trajectory

1292
01:05:20,547 --> 01:05:24,010
might have done
something good as well.

1293
01:05:24,010 --> 01:05:24,510
Yeah.

1294
01:05:24,510 --> 01:05:28,750
And then there's a question of,
when can we apply relabeling?

1295
01:05:28,750 --> 01:05:31,150
And in particular,
there's actually

1296
01:05:31,150 --> 01:05:38,110
restrictions on both the
set of MDPs or the MDPs

1297
01:05:38,110 --> 01:05:42,150
of the different tasks
and also on the algorithm

1298
01:05:42,150 --> 01:05:45,830
that you're using for
reinforcement learning.

1299
01:05:45,830 --> 01:05:49,950
So does anyone have
thoughts on what

1300
01:05:49,950 --> 01:05:54,750
has to be shared or not
shared for this algorithm

1301
01:05:54,750 --> 01:05:56,670
to make sense?

1302
01:05:56,670 --> 01:05:58,510
Yeah, so a shared
state action space.

1303
01:05:58,510 --> 01:06:09,150
So you're going to want a
shared state and action space.

1304
01:06:09,150 --> 01:06:14,130
Anything else do you want
that's shared and not shared?

1305
01:06:14,130 --> 01:06:14,630
Yeah.

1306
01:06:14,630 --> 01:06:15,910
So if you only have
one replay buffer,

1307
01:06:15,910 --> 01:06:18,160
then you want the dimensions
of your state and actions

1308
01:06:18,160 --> 01:06:22,710
to match and, generally, for
the state space and action

1309
01:06:22,710 --> 01:06:24,290
space to be the same.

1310
01:06:24,290 --> 01:06:24,790
Yeah.

1311
01:06:24,790 --> 01:06:27,350
So you probably want the
initial state distribution

1312
01:06:27,350 --> 01:06:28,750
to be similar.

1313
01:06:28,750 --> 01:06:43,340
So you want p of s1 to be
similar for different tasks.

1314
01:06:43,340 --> 01:06:46,780
Because if it's drastically
different, then probably,

1315
01:06:46,780 --> 01:06:49,460
sharing data won't be as useful.

1316
01:06:49,460 --> 01:06:52,140
So if one of them has dense
rewards and one of them

1317
01:06:52,140 --> 01:06:56,340
has sparse rewards,
that might be a problem.

1318
01:06:56,340 --> 01:06:59,220
That is true.

1319
01:06:59,220 --> 01:07:02,320
Although, that might be a
problem generally in multitask

1320
01:07:02,320 --> 01:07:02,820
RL.

1321
01:07:02,820 --> 01:07:05,922

1322
01:07:05,922 --> 01:07:07,380
But actually, in
this case, I don't

1323
01:07:07,380 --> 01:07:08,380
think it's going
to be especially

1324
01:07:08,380 --> 01:07:09,020
an issue in this case.

1325
01:07:09,020 --> 01:07:11,020
Because you are going
to be using the reward

1326
01:07:11,020 --> 01:07:12,120
function for the task.

1327
01:07:12,120 --> 01:07:13,920
But yeah, generally,
for multitask RL,

1328
01:07:13,920 --> 01:07:15,680
that makes things challenging.

1329
01:07:15,680 --> 01:07:16,180
Yeah.

1330
01:07:16,180 --> 01:07:18,530
So it's helpful for the
rewards to be the same scale.

1331
01:07:18,530 --> 01:07:19,530
What about the dynamics?

1332
01:07:19,530 --> 01:07:22,420

1333
01:07:22,420 --> 01:07:25,420
Should they be the same
or different across tasks?

1334
01:07:25,420 --> 01:07:26,480
Same, yeah.

1335
01:07:26,480 --> 01:07:28,558
So you want the
dynamics to be the same.

1336
01:07:28,558 --> 01:07:30,100
And that's actually
really important.

1337
01:07:30,100 --> 01:07:33,313
Because you're not
relabeling the dynamics here.

1338
01:07:33,313 --> 01:07:35,230
And because you're not
relabeling the dynamics

1339
01:07:35,230 --> 01:07:40,445
if they're different
across tasks, then yeah,

1340
01:07:40,445 --> 01:07:41,570
it's going to be a problem.

1341
01:07:41,570 --> 01:07:44,450
And so this is only
applicable-- because you're

1342
01:07:44,450 --> 01:07:46,970
teaching the model and
sharing data with the dynamics

1343
01:07:46,970 --> 01:07:49,610
from the other task, it's really
important for the dynamics

1344
01:07:49,610 --> 01:07:51,970
to be the same.

1345
01:07:51,970 --> 01:07:57,210
So the dynamics
should be the same.

1346
01:07:57,210 --> 01:08:00,090
And then this is also most
useful if the reward functions

1347
01:08:00,090 --> 01:08:01,170
are different.

1348
01:08:01,170 --> 01:08:04,930
Otherwise, the data is
already in the buffer

1349
01:08:04,930 --> 01:08:07,810
with the original
reward function.

1350
01:08:07,810 --> 01:08:13,210
My last question is, do you
need this to be an off-policy RL

1351
01:08:13,210 --> 01:08:16,220
algorithm, or is it OK
if this is on policy?

1352
01:08:16,220 --> 01:08:19,490

1353
01:08:19,490 --> 01:08:21,290
Any thoughts on that?

1354
01:08:21,290 --> 01:08:22,290
We'd like to have cover.

1355
01:08:22,290 --> 01:08:23,910
So off policy is generally good.

1356
01:08:23,910 --> 01:08:25,649
Yeah.

1357
01:08:25,649 --> 01:08:31,920
So the action here--

1358
01:08:31,920 --> 01:08:35,560
this was the action that
was taken by the policy

1359
01:08:35,560 --> 01:08:38,439
when conditioned on zi.

1360
01:08:38,439 --> 01:08:40,479
And when conditioned
on zj, the policy

1361
01:08:40,479 --> 01:08:42,439
might have done
something different.

1362
01:08:42,439 --> 01:08:44,439
And so this data
that we're relabeling

1363
01:08:44,439 --> 01:08:47,240
is going to be off-policy data.

1364
01:08:47,240 --> 01:08:51,200
And so that means that when
we're updating pi theta,

1365
01:08:51,200 --> 01:08:53,880
we really need to be using
an off-policy algorithm

1366
01:08:53,880 --> 01:08:57,240
because this data is not
following the policy conditioned

1367
01:08:57,240 --> 01:08:59,359
on zj.

1368
01:08:59,359 --> 01:09:00,180
Yeah, exactly.

1369
01:09:00,180 --> 01:09:02,359
So if you're going to be
using importance weights,

1370
01:09:02,359 --> 01:09:04,600
you need to store
which policy collected

1371
01:09:04,600 --> 01:09:06,920
the data in the first place.

1372
01:09:06,920 --> 01:09:10,380
Well, actually, sorry.

1373
01:09:10,380 --> 01:09:11,420
Let me think about that.

1374
01:09:11,420 --> 01:09:16,640

1375
01:09:16,640 --> 01:09:20,420
This data is actually changed
from the original data.

1376
01:09:20,420 --> 01:09:24,939
And so actually, for
the importance weights,

1377
01:09:24,939 --> 01:09:29,960
you'll actually want to use
zj, not zi, as the old policy.

1378
01:09:29,960 --> 01:09:32,439
Because you're actually kind
of creating synthetic data

1379
01:09:32,439 --> 01:09:35,960
that is as if you were using zj.

1380
01:09:35,960 --> 01:09:36,460
So yeah.

1381
01:09:36,460 --> 01:09:37,800
Sorry, I misspoke.

1382
01:09:37,800 --> 01:09:41,319
So you don't need to store
which policy it came from.

1383
01:09:41,319 --> 01:09:42,580
OK.

1384
01:09:42,580 --> 01:09:43,080
Oh.

1385
01:09:43,080 --> 01:09:45,543
The other thing is, you need
the reward function form here.

1386
01:09:45,543 --> 01:09:47,960
You need to actually compute
the rewards for the new task.

1387
01:09:47,960 --> 01:09:50,040
That's also important as well.

1388
01:09:50,040 --> 01:09:52,407
Or you need to have learned
the reward function.

1389
01:09:52,407 --> 01:09:53,740
So you need the reward function.

1390
01:09:53,740 --> 01:09:55,157
The dynamics has
to be consistent.

1391
01:09:55,157 --> 01:09:58,080
And you have to be using
an off-policy algorithm.

1392
01:09:58,080 --> 01:10:00,460
And then lastly is the
goal-conditioned version.

1393
01:10:00,460 --> 01:10:02,560
This is basically
the same as before,

1394
01:10:02,560 --> 01:10:05,320
but our reward function is
now this negative distance

1395
01:10:05,320 --> 01:10:06,760
between states.

1396
01:10:06,760 --> 01:10:08,800
And for continuous
states, this could

1397
01:10:08,800 --> 01:10:10,220
be like mean squared error.

1398
01:10:10,220 --> 01:10:12,520
It could be a distance
in embedding space.

1399
01:10:12,520 --> 01:10:16,520
Or it could be a 0, 1
loss within some epsilon

1400
01:10:16,520 --> 01:10:19,630
ball of the goal state.

1401
01:10:19,630 --> 01:10:29,000

